// Nifla front — the Nifla toolchain's front end, written in
// Nifla itself (Stones 47–50, the first stones of the
// all-Nifla port: the lexer and parser in Stones 47–48,
// the checker beginning in Stone 50).
//
// Run it the way the toolchain's front half would be run:
//
//     nifla-front tokens <path>
//     nifla-front tree <path>
//     nifla-front check <path>
//     nifla-front check-bare <path>
//     nifla-front check-guest <path>
//
// Stone 50 adds the check modes: lex, parse, then the
// checker (the section below the tree dump), printing
// exactly "checked" for an accepted program. In this
// stone all three check modes run the one hosted walk —
// the bare/guest tool doors and the bare floor-text rule
// land in Stone 51.
//
// 'tokens' reads the file at <path> and prints its tokens,
// one per line, in this dump:
//
//     number <VALUE> @<line>     VALUE written the way Nifla
//                                writes numbers for people
//     text "<ESC>" @<line>
//     name <word> @<line>
//     keyword <word> @<line>
//     symbol <sym> @<line>
//     floor "<ESC of asm>" @<line>
//     end @<line>
//
// ESC replaces, in this order: backslash -> \\, " -> \",
// newline -> \n, tab -> \t, carriage return -> \r; every other
// character passes through as it stands. The escaping is an
// explicit character loop (escText below), mirrored by the
// proof oracle's own loop — no library escaping on either side.
//
// 'tree' lexes and parses the file and prints the parse
// tree as ONE line (the format is written out in full above
// the tree-dump section below, and mirrored by the oracle).
// Leaving, both modes: 0 on success (the dump on the screen,
// nothing on the error channel); 1 on a lex refusal or — in
// 'tree' — a parse refusal (nothing on the screen,
// "Line N: <message>" on the error channel); 2 when the file
// itself can't be read (a plain message on the error
// channel).
//
// The lexer below is the self-hosted interpreter's lexer
// (selfhost/nifla-in-nifla.nifla) lifted out and made to stand
// on its own — then corrected wherever the self-host's habits
// diverge from the Swift lexer (src/Lexer.swift), because the
// standard for this program is identity with Swift, token for
// token and refusal for refusal. The corrections:
//
//   * Pending tokens: Swift's tokenize() drains the pending
//     queue before each nextToken() call, nextToken() itself
//     never looks at the queue, the floor-values collection
//     loop calls nextToken() directly, and a completed
//     floor-with-values ASSIGNS the queue (discarding whatever
//     a nested floor-with-values stranded there). So this
//     lexer has a raw next-token function (lexRaw) with no
//     pending logic in it — comment handling loops back to
//     the raw function's top instead of recursing (the same
//     walk, without spending call depth), collection reads
//     through the raw function — and lexAll's loop does the
//     draining.
//   * The missing-'{' refusal after floor values is spoken at
//     the 'floor' word's line (Swift's startLine), not at
//     whatever line the values wandered to.
//   * A floor block's closing line is told apart the way
//     Swift tells it: trimmed of spaces and tabs (and the
//     other space separators Swift's CharacterSet.whitespaces
//     holds) — a carriage return is NOT trimmed, so a line
//     holding '}' and a carriage return is an instruction
//     line, not a closing line. (Whitespace skipping between
//     tokens still skips space, tab, carriage return and
//     newline, exactly as Swift's nextToken does.)
//   * Numbers are read by a dedicated reader (numberFromDigits
//     below), not by the self-host's digit fold and not by
//     asnumber: the fold drifts on long digit strings, and
//     asnumber refuses overflow where Swift's lexer reads a
//     400-digit literal as the number inf. The reader keeps an
//     exact whole-number mantissa while the keeping is exact,
//     carries the next digits as a second, smaller mantissa,
//     and scales the pair by exact powers of ten with every
//     step's rounding error carried along (Dekker's
//     two-product), never rounded away. It matches Swift's
//     correctly rounded reading on the whole proved corpus
//     and the fixture set in front-examples/; it is not a
//     proof for every conceivable 17+-digit pattern — that
//     would take arbitrary precision — and any pattern found
//     to miss by an ulp belongs in front-examples/BOUNDARY.txt
//     with its pattern named, never silently absorbed.
//   * Word characters: Swift takes any Unicode letter or
//     number into a word. Nifla gives a program no way to ask
//     a character for its code point, so the full Unicode
//     tables cannot be written in the language — a finding,
//     not something to fake. This lexer's letter test covers
//     ASCII and the Latin-1 supplement letters (the
//     LATIN1LETTERS string below); digits stay ASCII-only.
//     Words written in letters beyond Latin-1, and non-ASCII
//     digits, diverge from Swift for that reason and are
//     named, with fixtures, in front-examples/BOUNDARY.txt.
//
// Stone 48 adds the parser: the self-host's parser lifted
// the same way and corrected to identity with the Swift
// parser (src/Parser.swift) — the statement fallthrough and
// the 'attempt' missing-'{' refusal in Swift's exact words
// and order, the 'if it fails' shape tested by clamped
// lookahead without consuming and refused in one message at
// the 'if' token's line, and floor expressions read the
// Swift way (the argument reading called unconditionally,
// the block asked for afterwards in Swift's words, the
// statement road going through parseExpression as Swift's
// does); every other refusal was checked word by word
// against Parser.swift as it was lifted and already
// matched. run-front-lexer.sh proves the token identity
// and run-front-parser.sh the tree identity against the
// Swift front end (through the proof-only oracle,
// tools/front-oracle.swift) over the whole corpus: every
// example suite, the self-host and feathercheck sources,
// every nifla block in PRIMER.md and TUTORIAL.md, the
// Cardnull OS 2 sources, and the fixtures.
//
// Part of Nifla, a public-domain programming language by Tyler
// Kovalevich. See the Unlicense in the LICENSE file at the root
// of this repository.

// ---------- small shared helpers ----------

// Every lexer function hands back a result instead of failing
// — Nifla has no errors to throw:
//   { ok: true, value: ... }        — it worked
//   { ok: false, message: "..." }   — the plain-words error,
//                                     already wearing its
//                                     "Line N:" (it also
//                                     carries "bare" and
//                                     "line", as the
//                                     self-host's results do)
make errAt(line, message) {
    give { ok: false, message: "Line " + line + ": " + message, bare: message, line: line }
}

make okVal(value) {
    give { ok: true, value: value }
}

// Is this character a digit, a letter, or part of a name?
// Digits are ASCII only (see the header: Swift also takes
// non-ASCII digits into a number and then refuses the number
// as unreadable — a divergence named in BOUNDARY.txt).
// The empty text — lexPeek's way of saying the end of the
// program — is none of these: find() reports an empty piece
// as found at 0, so without the guard the end of the program
// would read as a digit and a letter both, and the reading
// loops would walk past the end forever. Swift's peek()
// hands back nothing there and its loops simply stop; the
// guard is what makes these tests stop the same way.
make isDigitCh(c) {
    if c == "" {
        give false
    }
    give find("0123456789", c) >= 0
}

make digitVal(c) {
    give find("0123456789", c)
}

// The Latin-1 supplement letters: U+00C0–U+00D6, U+00D8–U+00F6,
// U+00F8–U+00FF — 62 characters. (The two signs among them,
// × U+00D7 and ÷ U+00F7, are not letters and are not here.)
let LATIN1LETTERS = "ÀÁÂÃÄÅÆÇÈÉÊËÌÍÎÏÐÑÒÓÔÕÖØÙÚÛÜÝÞßàáâãäåæçèéêëìíîïðñòóôõöøùúûüýþÿ"

make isLetterCh(c) {
    if c == "" {
        give false
    }
    if find("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ", c) >= 0 {
        give true
    }
    give find(LATIN1LETTERS, c) >= 0
}

make isNameCh(c) {
    if isLetterCh(c) {
        give true
    }
    if isDigitCh(c) {
        give true
    }
    give c == "_"
}

// A number written the way Nifla writes numbers for people —
// the host's own rendering, reached by joining the number onto
// text. (On the Swift side this is formatNumber; the host's
// number-to-text is that very function, so the two agree.)
make numText(n) {
    give "" + n
}

// Where a name lives in a names list, or -1 when it isn't
// there. (The parser asks it of parameter names, object
// field names, and its table of operator signs.)
make nameIndex(names, name) {
    let i = 0
    while i < length(names) {
        if names[i] == name {
            give i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    give -1
}

// ---------- the number reader ----------

// 10^k, for a small whole k (0 to 22 in every use here). Each
// power up to 10^22 is a whole number a Double holds exactly,
// and each step multiplies by ten exactly, so the power this
// gives back is exact.
make pow10n(k) {
    let p = 1
    let i = 0
    while i < k {
        p = p * 10
        i = i + 1
    }
    give p
}

// x scaled by 10^e, the brief's way: while e is 22 or more,
// multiply by 1e22 and take 22 off e; then multiply by the
// remaining exact power (e at most 21). Downward the same,
// dividing. Overflow on the way up gives inf, as Swift's
// reading of an enormous literal does.
make scalePow10(x, e) {
    let v = x
    let ee = e
    let p22 = pow10n(22)
    while ee >= 22 {
        v = v * p22
        ee = ee - 22
    }
    if ee > 0 {
        v = v * pow10n(ee)
    }
    while ee <= -22 {
        v = v / p22
        ee = ee + 22
    }
    if ee < 0 {
        v = v / pow10n(0 - ee)
    }
    give v
}

// Splitting a number into a head and a tail that add back to
// it exactly (Veltkamp's split, the constant 2^27 + 1), and
// the exact product of two numbers as a head and a tail
// (Dekker's two-product): prod.hi is the rounded product and
// prod.lo is its exact error, so hi + lo is the true product.
// The number reader scales by pairs like these so that no
// step's rounding is ever lost — a rounded head with its
// error carried along, never a rounded head alone.
make splitNum(x) {
    let c = 134217729 * x
    let hi = c - (c - x)
    give { hi: hi, lo: x - hi }
}

make twoProduct(a, b) {
    let p = a * b
    let sa = splitNum(a)
    let sb = splitNum(b)
    let err = ((sa.hi * sb.hi - p) + sa.hi * sb.lo + sa.lo * sb.hi) + sa.lo * sb.lo
    give { hi: p, lo: err }
}

// The pair (h, l) — h the head, l its small correction, the
// two together worth h + l — scaled by 10^e, pair-corrected
// at every chunk: a multiply carries the product's exact
// error into the tail, a divide carries the division's exact
// remainder (found through the two-product of the quotient)
// into the tail. While the head stays in the wide safe band
// between 1e-290 and 1e290 every one of those steps is
// exact-headed; outside the band — a value within reach of
// overflow, or down among the subnormals — the remaining
// scale is applied plainly, and a literal whose true value
// lives out there can miss Swift's reading in its last ulps.
// No literal in the corpus or the fixtures lives out there.
make scalePairPow10(h, l, e) {
    let head = h
    let tail = l
    let ee = e
    let p22 = pow10n(22)
    let safeHi = scalePow10(1, 290)
    let safeLo = scalePow10(1, -290)
    while ee >= 22 {
        if head > safeHi {
            give scalePow10(head + tail, ee)
        }
        let tp = twoProduct(head, p22)
        tail = tail * p22 + tp.lo
        head = tp.hi
        ee = ee - 22
    }
    if ee > 0 {
        let p = pow10n(ee)
        if head <= safeHi {
            let tp = twoProduct(head, p)
            tail = tail * p + tp.lo
            head = tp.hi
        } else {
            head = scalePow10(head + tail, ee)
            tail = 0
        }
        ee = 0
    }
    while ee <= -22 {
        if head < safeLo {
            give scalePow10(head + tail, ee)
        }
        let q = head / p22
        let tp = twoProduct(q, p22)
        let r = (head - tp.hi) - tp.lo
        tail = (r + tail) / p22
        head = q
        ee = ee + 22
    }
    if ee < 0 {
        let p = pow10n(0 - ee)
        if head >= safeLo {
            let q = head / p
            let tp = twoProduct(q, p)
            let r = (head - tp.hi) - tp.lo
            tail = (r + tail) / p
            head = q
        } else {
            head = scalePow10(head + tail, ee)
            tail = 0
        }
    }
    give head + tail
}

// A number built out of its digit text: `digits` is the whole
// and fraction digits joined into one string, and `fracCount`
// says how many of them stood after the decimal point, so the
// value is digits × 10^(−fracCount) before the digits that
// don't fit are accounted. The first mantissa is accumulated
// while the accumulation stays exact — digits are taken while
// m ≤ 900719925474098 before the step, so m*10+d stays under
// 2^53 and exact; every digit not taken adds 1 to the
// exponent. The next fifteen digits are accumulated the same
// way into a second mantissa, held as the fraction f of one
// unit in the first mantissa's last place, and the pair
// (m, f) is scaled together by scalePairPow10, so the tail
// is never rounded away against a head that was rounded the
// other way. What remains beyond the second mantissa is
// worth less than 1e-30 of the whole and cannot move the
// result; a pattern that still misses belongs in
// front-examples/BOUNDARY.txt with its pattern named.
make numberFromDigits(digits, fracCount) {
    let total = length(digits)
    let m = 0
    let taken = 0
    let i = 0
    let taking = true
    while taking {
        if i >= total {
            taking = false
        }
        if taking {
            if m > 900719925474098 {
                taking = false
            }
        }
        if taking {
            m = m * 10 + digitVal(digits[i])
            taken = taken + 1
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    let m2 = 0
    let taken2 = 0
    taking = true
    while taking {
        if i >= total {
            taking = false
        }
        if taking {
            if taken2 >= 15 {
                taking = false
            }
        }
        if taking {
            m2 = m2 * 10 + digitVal(digits[i])
            taken2 = taken2 + 1
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    let eMain = (total - taken) - fracCount
    if m2 == 0 {
        give scalePow10(m, eMain)
    }
    give scalePairPow10(m, m2 / pow10n(taken2), eMain)
}

// ---------- the lexer ----------
// Turns the program's text into tokens, remembering the line
// each came from. The state walks the text as a list of
// one-character texts.

let gChars = []
let gPos = 0
let gLine = 1

// Tokens already read and waiting their turn (Stone 9): when
// a floor block is handed values — floor(value, ...) { ... }
// — the values' tokens are read while the word 'floor' is
// being read, so the block itself can be captured right after
// them; they wait here, behind the 'floor' keyword token, and
// lexAll hands them out before any new reading. A completed
// floor-with-values ASSIGNS this queue, as Swift assigns
// pendingTokens — whatever a nested floor-with-values left
// stranded here is discarded, exactly as Swift discards it.
let gPending = []

make lexPeek() {
    if gPos < length(gChars) {
        give gChars[gPos]
    }
    give ""
}

make lexPeek2() {
    if gPos + 1 < length(gChars) {
        give gChars[gPos + 1]
    }
    give ""
}

// The carriage-return character, held in a text of its own. It
// is written here as the character itself, between the quotes,
// because Nifla text has no escape for it.
let CRCHAR = ""

make isSpaceCh(c) {
    if c == " " {
        give true
    }
    if c == "\t" {
        give true
    }
    if c == CRCHAR {
        give true
    }
    give false
}

// The characters Swift's CharacterSet.whitespaces trims from
// a floor block's closing line: the tab and the Unicode space
// separators. A carriage return is NOT among them — that is
// the whole point (see the header). The space itself is
// tested alongside, in isFloorTrimCh.
let FLOORTRIM = "               　"

make isFloorTrimCh(c) {
    if c == " " {
        give true
    }
    if c == "\t" {
        give true
    }
    give find(FLOORTRIM, c) >= 0
}

// Text with the floor-closing trim taken off both ends — for
// telling a floor block's closing line from an instruction.
make trimFloorLine(t) {
    let lo = 0
    let scanning = true
    while scanning {
        if lo >= length(t) {
            scanning = false
        }
        if scanning {
            if isFloorTrimCh(t[lo]) {
                lo = lo + 1
            } else {
                scanning = false
            }
        }
    }
    let hi = length(t)
    scanning = true
    while scanning {
        if hi <= lo {
            scanning = false
        }
        if scanning {
            if isFloorTrimCh(t[hi - 1]) {
                hi = hi - 1
            } else {
                scanning = false
            }
        }
    }
    give part(t, lo, hi - lo)
}

make isKeywordWord(w) {
    let words = ["let", "say", "if", "else", "while", "repeat", "for", "in", "make", "give", "true", "false", "floor", "attempt", "nothing"]
    for word in words {
        if word == w {
            give true
        }
    }
    give false
}

// Takes the whole program's text apart into tokens:
// { ok: true, value: [tokens] }, or the lexer's plain-words
// error. The pending queue is drained here, before each raw
// reading — Swift's tokenize() shape.
make lexAll(source) {
    gChars = []
    for c in source {
        append(gChars, c)
    }
    gPos = 0
    gLine = 1
    let tokens = []
    let done = false
    while done == false {
        if length(gPending) > 0 {
            let waiting = gPending[0]
            removeat(gPending, 0)
            append(tokens, waiting)
            if waiting.kind == "end" {
                done = true
            }
        } else {
            let r = lexRaw()
            if r.ok == false {
                give r
            }
            append(tokens, r.value)
            if r.value.kind == "end" {
                done = true
            }
        }
    }
    give okVal(tokens)
}

// One token, read raw from wherever the lexer stands —
// Swift's nextToken(). The pending queue is never consulted
// here. Swift's comment handling calls nextToken() again for
// the token after the comment; this one loops back to the top
// instead, which is the same walk and spends no call depth:
// cardnull.nifla opens with a 786-line run of comments, and
// the host's five-hundred-call guard would have stopped a
// recursion that deep, while Swift's native stack sails on.
make lexRaw() {
    let again = true
    while again {
        again = false
        // Skip spaces and blank lines; remember line numbers.
        let skipping = true
        while skipping {
            let c = lexPeek()
            if c == "\n" {
                gLine = gLine + 1
                gPos = gPos + 1
            } else {
                if isSpaceCh(c) {
                    gPos = gPos + 1
                } else {
                    skipping = false
                }
            }
        }
        let startLine = gLine
        let c = lexPeek()
        if c == "" {
            give okVal({ kind: "end", line: startLine })
        }
        // A // comment runs to the end of the line; when one
        // is eaten, go around again for the token after it.
        if c == "/" {
            if lexPeek2() == "/" {
                let eating = true
                while eating {
                    let ch = lexPeek()
                    if ch == "\n" {
                        eating = false
                    }
                    if ch == "" {
                        eating = false
                    }
                    if eating {
                        gPos = gPos + 1
                    }
                }
                again = true
            }
        }
        if again == false {
            if isDigitCh(c) {
                give lexNumberTok(startLine)
            }
            if c == "\"" {
                give lexTextTok(startLine)
            }
            if isLetterCh(c) {
                give lexWordTok(startLine)
            }
            if c == "_" {
                give lexWordTok(startLine)
            }
            // Two-character symbols first, so "=" and "==" stay distinct.
            let pair = c + lexPeek2()
            if pair == "==" {
                gPos = gPos + 2
                give okVal({ kind: "symbol", symbol: pair, line: startLine })
            }
            if pair == "!=" {
                gPos = gPos + 2
                give okVal({ kind: "symbol", symbol: pair, line: startLine })
            }
            if pair == "<=" {
                gPos = gPos + 2
                give okVal({ kind: "symbol", symbol: pair, line: startLine })
            }
            if pair == ">=" {
                gPos = gPos + 2
                give okVal({ kind: "symbol", symbol: pair, line: startLine })
            }
            if pair == "->" {
                gPos = gPos + 2
                give okVal({ kind: "symbol", symbol: pair, line: startLine })
            }
            if find("+-*/(){}[]=<>.,:", c) >= 0 {
                gPos = gPos + 1
                give okVal({ kind: "symbol", symbol: c, line: startLine })
            }
            if c == "!" {
                give errAt(startLine, "I found a '!' on its own. To say 'not equal', write '!=' — an exclamation mark followed by an equals sign.")
            }
            give errAt(startLine, "I don't understand the character '" + c + "'. Nifla programs are made of words, numbers, text in \"quotes\", and the signs + - * / ( ) { } [ ] , . : = == != < <= > >=.")
        }
    }
    // Unreachable: every pass through the loop either gives a
    // token or an error, or goes around again for a comment.
    give errAt(gLine, "I don't understand the character ''. Nifla programs are made of words, numbers, text in \"quotes\", and the signs + - * / ( ) { } [ ] , . : = == != < <= > >=.")
}
make lexNumberTok(startLine) {
    let whole = ""
    let eating = true
    while eating {
        let c = lexPeek()
        if isDigitCh(c) {
            whole = whole + c
            gPos = gPos + 1
        } else {
            eating = false
        }
    }
    // A decimal point counts only when a digit follows it.
    let frac = ""
    if lexPeek() == "." {
        if isDigitCh(lexPeek2()) {
            gPos = gPos + 1
            eating = true
            while eating {
                let c = lexPeek()
                if isDigitCh(c) {
                    frac = frac + c
                    gPos = gPos + 1
                } else {
                    eating = false
                }
            }
        }
    }
    give okVal({ kind: "number", num: numberFromDigits(whole + frac, length(frac)), line: startLine })
}

make lexTextTok(startLine) {
    gPos = gPos + 1  // the opening quote
    let value = ""
    let eating = true
    while eating {
        let c = lexPeek()
        if c == "" {
            eating = false
            give errAt(startLine, "This text never ends. Add a closing \" to finish it.")
        }
        if c == "\"" {
            gPos = gPos + 1
            eating = false
            give okVal({ kind: "text", text: value, line: startLine })
        }
        if c == "\n" {
            eating = false
            give errAt(startLine, "This text never ends — it runs into the end of the line. Add a closing \" to finish it.")
        }
        if c == "\\" {
            gPos = gPos + 1
            let esc = lexPeek()
            if esc == "" {
                eating = false
                give errAt(startLine, "This text never ends. Add a closing \" to finish it.")
            }
            if esc == "n" {
                value = value + "\n"
            } else {
                if esc == "t" {
                    value = value + "\t"
                } else {
                    if esc == "\"" {
                        value = value + "\""
                    } else {
                        if esc == "\\" {
                            value = value + "\\"
                        } else {
                            eating = false
                            give errAt(startLine, "I don't know the escape '\\" + esc + "' inside text. The ones I know are \\n (new line), \\t (tab), \\\" (a quote), and \\\\ (a backslash).")
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            gPos = gPos + 1
        } else {
            value = value + c
            gPos = gPos + 1
        }
    }
    give errAt(startLine, "This text never ends. Add a closing \" to finish it.")
}

make lexWordTok(startLine) {
    let word = ""
    let eating = true
    while eating {
        let c = lexPeek()
        if isNameCh(c) {
            word = word + c
            gPos = gPos + 1
        } else {
            eating = false
        }
    }
    if word == "floor" {
        // A floor block: 'floor' followed on the same line by
        // '{' opens a stretch of raw lines — processor
        // instructions, taken exactly as written — until a
        // line holding only '}'. Those lines are never lexed
        // as Nifla. Since Stone 9 the block can be handed
        // values first: 'floor' then '( ... )' then '{'. The
        // values are ordinary Nifla, so their tokens are read
        // now — through the raw reading, as Swift's lexWord
        // calls nextToken() directly — and wait in gPending
        // behind the 'floor' keyword token this gives back.
        let savedPos = gPos
        let skipping = true
        while skipping {
            let c = lexPeek()
            if c == " " {
                gPos = gPos + 1
            } else {
                if c == "\t" {
                    gPos = gPos + 1
                } else {
                    skipping = false
                }
            }
        }
        if lexPeek() == "(" {
            let collected = []
            let depth = 0
            let collecting = true
            while collecting {
                let r = lexRaw()
                if r.ok == false {
                    give r
                }
                if r.value.kind == "end" {
                    give errAt(startLine, "The '(' after 'floor' is never closed. Close the values being handed to the block with ')', then give the block itself: floor(values) { ... }.")
                }
                append(collected, r.value)
                if r.value.kind == "symbol" {
                    if r.value.symbol == "(" {
                        depth = depth + 1
                    }
                    if r.value.symbol == ")" {
                        depth = depth - 1
                        if depth == 0 {
                            collecting = false
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            skipping = true
            while skipping {
                let c = lexPeek()
                if c == " " {
                    gPos = gPos + 1
                } else {
                    if c == "\t" {
                        gPos = gPos + 1
                    } else {
                        skipping = false
                    }
                }
            }
            if lexPeek() != "{" {
                give errAt(startLine, "After the values handed to 'floor' I need the block itself: '{' on the same line, then the processor instructions on the lines below, until a line holding only '}'.")
            }
            gPos = gPos + 1  // the '{'
            let rest = lexFloorRest(startLine)
            if rest.ok == false {
                give rest
            }
            gPending = collected
            append(gPending, rest.value)
            give okVal({ kind: "keyword", word: "floor", line: startLine })
        }
        if lexPeek() == "{" {
            gPos = gPos + 1  // the '{'
            give lexFloorRest(startLine)
        }
        gPos = savedPos
    }
    if isKeywordWord(word) {
        give okVal({ kind: "keyword", word: word, line: startLine })
    }
    give okVal({ kind: "name", name: word, line: startLine })
}

// The rest of a floor block, once its '{' has been read:
// nothing else on the opening line but an optional comment,
// then the raw lines, one per line, until a line holding
// only '}'. Gives back the floor token.
make lexFloorRest(startLine) {
    let skipping = true
    while skipping {
        let c = lexPeek()
        if c == " " {
            gPos = gPos + 1
        } else {
            if c == "\t" {
                gPos = gPos + 1
            } else {
                skipping = false
            }
        }
    }
    if lexPeek() == "/" {
        if lexPeek2() == "/" {
            let eatingComment = true
            while eatingComment {
                let ch = lexPeek()
                if ch == "\n" {
                    eatingComment = false
                }
                if ch == "" {
                    eatingComment = false
                }
                if eatingComment {
                    gPos = gPos + 1
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if lexPeek() == "\n" {
        gPos = gPos + 1
        gLine = gLine + 1
    } else {
        if lexPeek() != "" {
            give errAt(startLine, "After 'floor {' the processor instructions go on the lines below it, one per line, until a line holding only '}'. Take anything else off this line.")
        }
    }
    let asmLines = []
    let capturing = true
    while capturing {
        if lexPeek() == "" {
            give errAt(startLine, "This 'floor' block is never closed. Add a line holding only '}' to finish it.")
        }
        let lineText = ""
        let eatingLine = true
        while eatingLine {
            let ch = lexPeek()
            if ch == "\n" {
                eatingLine = false
            }
            if ch == "" {
                eatingLine = false
            }
            if eatingLine {
                lineText = lineText + ch
                gPos = gPos + 1
            }
        }
        if lexPeek() == "\n" {
            gPos = gPos + 1
            gLine = gLine + 1
        }
        if trimFloorLine(lineText) == "}" {
            capturing = false
        } else {
            append(asmLines, lineText)
        }
    }
    give okVal({ kind: "floor", asm: join(asmLines, "\n"), line: startLine })
}

// ---------- the parser ----------
// Reads the tokens and builds a tree of statements and
// expressions, as tagged objects:
//   statements: { type: "declare", name, kindWord, value, line }
//               { type: "assign", name, value, line }
//               { type: "assignTarget", target, value, line }
//               { type: "say", value, line }
//               { type: "if", condition, thenStmts, elseStmts, hasElse, line }
//               { type: "while", condition, body, line }
//               { type: "repeat", count, body, line }
//               { type: "for", name, over, body, line }
//               { type: "define", name, params, paramKinds, returnKind, body, line }
//               { type: "give", value, line }
//               { type: "attempt", body, recovery, line }
//               { type: "doCall", name, args, line }
//               { type: "doExpr", expr, line }
//   expressions: { type: "number", value, line }
//               { type: "text", value, line }
//               { type: "boolean", flag, line }
//               { type: "nothing", line }
//               { type: "variable", name, line }
//               { type: "call", name, args, line }
//               { type: "object", names, values, line }
//               { type: "list", items, line }
//               { type: "field", base, name, line }
//               { type: "index", base, index, line }
//               { type: "floor", asm, args, line }
//               { type: "negate", inner, line }
//               { type: "binary", op, left, right, line }
//
// Ported into the front end in Stone 48 and corrected to
// identity with the Swift parser (src/Parser.swift) wherever
// the self-host's habits diverged: the statement fallthrough
// and the 'attempt' missing-'{' refusal in Swift's exact
// words and order; the 'if it fails' shape tested by clamped
// lookahead without consuming, refused in one message at the
// 'if' token's line; and floor expressions read the Swift
// way — the argument reading called unconditionally, the
// block asked for afterwards in Swift's words, the statement
// road going through parseExpression as Swift's does. Every
// other refusal was checked word by word against
// Parser.swift as it was lifted and already matched.

let gToks = []
let gP = 0

make pCur() {
    give gToks[gP]
}

make pNext() {
    if gP + 1 < length(gToks) {
        give gToks[gP + 1]
    }
    give gToks[gP]
}

// The token k places ahead of the current one, clamped to
// the last token — Swift's 'attempt' lookahead reads pos+1,
// pos+2 and pos+3 each clamped the same way, and consumes
// none of them by the reading.
make pPeekAt(k) {
    let idx = gP + k
    if idx >= length(gToks) {
        idx = length(gToks) - 1
    }
    give gToks[idx]
}

make pAdvance() {
    if gP < length(gToks) - 1 {
        gP = gP + 1
    }
}

make pIsSym(s) {
    let t = pCur()
    if t.kind == "symbol" {
        give t.symbol == s
    }
    give false
}

make pIsKw(w) {
    let t = pCur()
    if t.kind == "keyword" {
        give t.word == w
    }
    give false
}

// How a token reads in an error message.
make pSpoken(t) {
    if t.kind == "number" {
        give "the number " + numText(t.num)
    }
    if t.kind == "text" {
        give "the text \"" + t.text + "\""
    }
    if t.kind == "name" {
        give "the name '" + t.name + "'"
    }
    if t.kind == "keyword" {
        give "'" + t.word + "'"
    }
    if t.kind == "symbol" {
        give "'" + t.symbol + "'"
    }
    if t.kind == "floor" {
        give "a 'floor' block"
    }
    give "the end of the program"
}

make parseProgramAll(tokens) {
    gToks = tokens
    gP = 0
    let stmts = []
    let going = true
    while going {
        if pCur().kind == "end" {
            going = false
        }
        if going {
            let r = parseStatement()
            if r.ok == false {
                give r
            }
            append(stmts, r.value)
        }
    }
    give okVal(stmts)
}

make parseBlock(openLine) {
    let stmts = []
    let going = true
    while going {
        if pIsSym("}") {
            going = false
        }
        if going {
            if pCur().kind == "end" {
                give errAt(openLine, "This '{' is never closed. Add a '}' to finish the block it starts.")
            }
            let r = parseStatement()
            if r.ok == false {
                give r
            }
            append(stmts, r.value)
        }
    }
    pAdvance()  // the closing '}'
    give okVal(stmts)
}

// Stone 18: reads one kind word where an annotation puts
// one — after a ':' in a 'let' or a 'make', or after the
// arrow of a 'make'. The words are the checker's own kind
// vocabulary; anything else is refused in plain words, in
// the Swift parser's own wording.
make isKindWord(word) {
    if word == "number" {
        give true
    }
    if word == "text" {
        give true
    }
    if word == "boolean" {
        give true
    }
    if word == "list" {
        give true
    }
    if word == "object" {
        give true
    }
    if word == "bytes" {
        give true
    }
    if word == "nothing" {
        give true
    }
    give false
}

make parseKindWord(after) {
    let t = pCur()
    // Stone 19: 'nothing' is a word of the language now, so
    // it arrives as a keyword token, not a name — it is a
    // kind word all the same, in the plain position.
    if t.kind == "keyword" {
        if t.word == "nothing" {
            pAdvance()
            give okVal("nothing")
        }
    }
    if t.kind != "name" {
        give errAt(t.line, "After the " + after + " I need a kind — one of: number, text, boolean, list, object, bytes, nothing. Instead I found " + pSpoken(t) + ".")
    }
    let word = t.name
    // Stone 19: 'maybe' may stand before a kind.
    if word == "maybe" {
        pAdvance()
        let t2 = pCur()
        if t2.kind == "keyword" {
            if t2.word == "nothing" {
                give errAt(t2.line, "'maybe nothing' says no more than 'nothing' itself — a value that may be nothing or nothing is just nothing. Write 'nothing' alone, or 'maybe' before a real kind, like: maybe number.")
            }
        }
        if t2.kind != "name" {
            give errAt(t2.line, "After 'maybe' I need a kind — one of: number, text, boolean, list, object, bytes. Instead I found " + pSpoken(t2) + ".")
        }
        let inner = t2.name
        if isKindWord(inner) == false {
            give errAt(t2.line, "I don't know a kind called '" + inner + "'. The kinds a value can be marked as are: number, text, boolean, list, object, bytes, nothing — and 'maybe' can stand before any of them but 'nothing', like: maybe number.")
        }
        pAdvance()
        give okVal("maybe " + inner)
    }
    if isKindWord(word) == false {
        give errAt(t.line, "I don't know a kind called '" + word + "'. The kinds a value can be marked as are: number, text, boolean, list, object, bytes, nothing.")
    }
    pAdvance()
    give okVal(word)
}

make parseStatement() {
    let token = pCur()

    // A floor block standing on its own.
    if token.kind == "floor" {
        pAdvance()
        give okVal({ type: "doExpr", expr: { type: "floor", asm: token.asm, args: [], line: token.line }, line: token.line })
    }

    // A floor block handed values, standing on its own:
    // floor(values) { ... } — the same expression, run for
    // what it does to the memory it was handed. Swift reads
    // it through parseExpression, and so does this port: the
    // expression road leads to the same floor case in
    // parseAtom.
    if pIsKw("floor") {
        let f = parseExpression()
        if f.ok == false {
            give f
        }
        give okVal({ type: "doExpr", expr: f.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("let") {
        pAdvance()
        if pCur().kind != "name" {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'let' I need a name for the new variable, like: let years = 29. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let name = pCur().name
        pAdvance()
        let kindWord = ""
        if pIsSym(":") {
            pAdvance()
            let k = parseKindWord("':' after 'let " + name + "'")
            if k.ok == false {
                give k
            }
            kindWord = k.value
        }
        if pIsSym("=") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'let " + name + "' I need an '=' and then a value, like: let " + name + " = 29. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        pAdvance()
        let v = parseExpression()
        if v.ok == false {
            give v
        }
        give okVal({ type: "declare", name: name, kindWord: kindWord, value: v.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("say") {
        pAdvance()
        let v = parseExpression()
        if v.ok == false {
            give v
        }
        give okVal({ type: "say", value: v.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("if") {
        pAdvance()
        let cond = parseExpression()
        if cond.ok == false {
            give cond
        }
        if pIsSym("{") == false {
            if pIsSym("=") {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "After the 'if' condition I expected '{' but found '='. To compare two things, use '==' — two equals signs. A single '=' sets a variable.")
            }
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After the 'if' condition I need a '{' to start the block that runs when it's true. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let openLine = pCur().line
        pAdvance()
        let thenR = parseBlock(openLine)
        if thenR.ok == false {
            give thenR
        }
        let elseStmts = []
        let hasElse = false
        if pIsKw("else") {
            pAdvance()
            if pIsSym("{") == false {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'else' I need a '{' to start the block that runs when the condition is false. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
            }
            let elseOpen = pCur().line
            pAdvance()
            let elseR = parseBlock(elseOpen)
            if elseR.ok == false {
                give elseR
            }
            elseStmts = elseR.value
            hasElse = true
        }
        give okVal({ type: "if", condition: cond.value, thenStmts: thenR.value, elseStmts: elseStmts, hasElse: hasElse, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("while") {
        pAdvance()
        let cond = parseExpression()
        if cond.ok == false {
            give cond
        }
        if pIsSym("{") == false {
            if pIsSym("=") {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "After the 'while' condition I expected '{' but found '='. To compare two things, use '==' — two equals signs. A single '=' sets a variable.")
            }
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After the 'while' condition I need a '{' to start the block that runs again and again while it's true. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let openLine = pCur().line
        pAdvance()
        let bodyR = parseBlock(openLine)
        if bodyR.ok == false {
            give bodyR
        }
        give okVal({ type: "while", condition: cond.value, body: bodyR.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("repeat") {
        pAdvance()
        let count = parseExpression()
        if count.ok == false {
            give count
        }
        if pIsSym("{") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After the 'repeat' count I need a '{' to start the block that runs that many times, like: repeat 3 { ... }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let openLine = pCur().line
        pAdvance()
        let bodyR = parseBlock(openLine)
        if bodyR.ok == false {
            give bodyR
        }
        give okVal({ type: "repeat", count: count.value, body: bodyR.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("for") {
        pAdvance()
        if pCur().kind != "name" {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'for' I need a name for each item the loop walks through, like: for item in items { ... }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let name = pCur().name
        pAdvance()
        if pIsKw("in") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'for " + name + "' I need 'in' and then the list or text to walk through, like: for " + name + " in items { ... }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        pAdvance()
        let over = parseExpression()
        if over.ok == false {
            give over
        }
        if pIsSym("{") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'for " + name + " in ...' I need a '{' to start the block that runs for each item. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let openLine = pCur().line
        pAdvance()
        let bodyR = parseBlock(openLine)
        if bodyR.ok == false {
            give bodyR
        }
        give okVal({ type: "for", name: name, over: over.value, body: bodyR.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("make") {
        pAdvance()
        if pCur().kind != "name" {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'make' I need a name for the new function, like: make greet(name) { ... }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let name = pCur().name
        pAdvance()
        if pIsSym("(") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After the function's name I need '(' and the names of the values it takes, like: make " + name + "(name) { ... }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        pAdvance()  // the '('
        let params = []
        let paramKinds = []
        if pIsSym(")") == false {
            let collecting = true
            while collecting {
                if pCur().kind != "name" {
                    give errAt(pCur().line, "Inside the parentheses of 'make " + name + "' I need names for the values, separated by commas, like: make " + name + "(a, b) { ... }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
                }
                let param = pCur().name
                if nameIndex(params, param) >= 0 {
                    give errAt(pCur().line, "The function '" + name + "' uses the name '" + param + "' twice in its list of values. Each value needs its own name.")
                }
                append(params, param)
                pAdvance()
                let pkind = ""
                if pIsSym(":") {
                    pAdvance()
                    let k = parseKindWord("':' after '" + param + "'")
                    if k.ok == false {
                        give k
                    }
                    pkind = k.value
                }
                append(paramKinds, pkind)
                if pIsSym(",") {
                    pAdvance()
                } else {
                    collecting = false
                }
            }
        }
        if pIsSym(")") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "I expected a ')' to close the list of values for '" + name + "', but I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        pAdvance()  // the ')'
        let returnKind = ""
        if pIsSym("->") {
            pAdvance()
            let k = parseKindWord("'->' in 'make " + name + "'")
            if k.ok == false {
                give k
            }
            returnKind = k.value
        }
        if pIsSym("{") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'make " + name + "(...)' I need a '{' to start the block that runs when the function is called. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let openLine = pCur().line
        pAdvance()
        let bodyR = parseBlock(openLine)
        if bodyR.ok == false {
            give bodyR
        }
        give okVal({ type: "define", name: name, params: params, paramKinds: paramKinds, returnKind: returnKind, body: bodyR.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("give") {
        pAdvance()
        let v = parseExpression()
        if v.ok == false {
            give v
        }
        give okVal({ type: "give", value: v.value, line: token.line })
    }

    if pIsKw("attempt") {
        pAdvance()
        if pIsSym("{") == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After 'attempt' I need a '{' to start the block that tries, like: attempt { ... } if it fails { ... }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
        }
        let openLine = pCur().line
        pAdvance()
        let bodyR = parseBlock(openLine)
        if bodyR.ok == false {
            give bodyR
        }
        // Swift's shape test: with the current token the
        // keyword 'if', the tokens one, two and three places
        // ahead — each read clamped to the last token, none
        // of them consumed by the test — must be the name
        // 'it', the name 'fails', and the symbol '{'. Any
        // miss is the one refusal below, spoken at the
        // current token's line however deep in the lookahead
        // the wrong token stands.
        let itTok = pPeekAt(1)
        let failsTok = pPeekAt(2)
        let braceTok = pPeekAt(3)
        let shape = false
        if pIsKw("if") {
            if itTok.kind == "name" {
                if itTok.name == "it" {
                    if failsTok.kind == "name" {
                        if failsTok.name == "fails" {
                            if braceTok.kind == "symbol" {
                                if braceTok.symbol == "{" {
                                    shape = true
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if shape == false {
            give errAt(pCur().line, "After an 'attempt' block I need 'if it fails' and a block saying what to do when something goes wrong, like: attempt { ... } if it fails { ... }.")
        }
        pAdvance()  // 'if'
        pAdvance()  // 'it'
        pAdvance()  // 'fails'
        let recoveryOpen = pCur().line
        pAdvance()  // '{
        let recoveryR = parseBlock(recoveryOpen)
        if recoveryR.ok == false {
            give recoveryR
        }
        give okVal({ type: "attempt", body: bodyR.value, recovery: recoveryR.value, line: token.line })
    }

    // Lines that start with a name: setting a variable, setting a
    // field or an item, or calling a function for what it does.
    if token.kind == "name" {
        let name = token.name
        let next = pNext()
        if next.kind == "symbol" {
            if next.symbol == "=" {
                pAdvance()  // the name
                pAdvance()  // the '='
                let v = parseExpression()
                if v.ok == false {
                    give v
                }
                give okVal({ type: "assign", name: name, value: v.value, line: token.line })
            }
            if next.symbol == "(" {
                pAdvance()  // the name
                let argsR = parseCallArgs(name)
                if argsR.ok == false {
                    give argsR
                }
                give okVal({ type: "doCall", name: name, args: argsR.value, line: token.line })
            }
            if next.symbol == "." {
                let targetR = parseTargetChain(name, token.line)
                if targetR.ok == false {
                    give targetR
                }
                let v = parseExpression()
                if v.ok == false {
                    give v
                }
                give okVal({ type: "assignTarget", target: targetR.value, value: v.value, line: token.line })
            }
            if next.symbol == "[" {
                let targetR = parseTargetChain(name, token.line)
                if targetR.ok == false {
                    give targetR
                }
                let v = parseExpression()
                if v.ok == false {
                    give v
                }
                give okVal({ type: "assignTarget", target: targetR.value, value: v.value, line: token.line })
            }
        }
        give errAt(token.line, "I found the name '" + name + "' at the start of a line, but a line needs to do something: make a variable with 'let " + name + " = ...', set it with '" + name + " = ...', set a field with '" + name + ".field = ...', set an item with '" + name + "[0] = ...', show it with 'say " + name + "', or call a function with '" + name + "(...)'.")
    }

    give errAt(token.line, "I expected a line to start with 'let', 'say', 'if', 'while', 'repeat', 'for', 'make', 'give', 'attempt', or the name of a variable to set — but I found " + pSpoken(token) + ".")
}

// A floor expression: the 'floor' keyword, the values handed
// to the block, and the block itself (which the lexer
// captured whole, as one token). Swift calls its argument
// reading unconditionally here: that reading advances first,
// taking whatever token stands where '(' should be, reads
// values while the token after them is not ')', and only
// then asks for ')' in its own words — so a bare 'floor'
// with nothing after it is refused by the value reading that
// follows, in the atom refusal's words, exactly as Swift
// refuses it. Only after the values does Swift ask for the
// block itself, in the words below.
make parseFloorExpr() {
    let token = pCur()
    pAdvance()  // the 'floor' keyword
    let argsR = parseCallArgs("floor")
    if argsR.ok == false {
        give argsR
    }
    if pCur().kind != "floor" {
        give errAt(pCur().line, "After the values handed to 'floor' I need the block itself: '{' on the same line, then the processor instructions. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
    }
    let block = pCur()
    pAdvance()
    give okVal({ type: "floor", asm: block.asm, args: argsR.value, line: token.line })
}

// A target chain after a leading name — ".field" and "[...]"
// steps — ending at the '=' of a setting line. The current token
// is the name; the chain and the '=' are consumed here, so the
// caller parses the value next.
make parseTargetChain(name, line) {
    pAdvance()  // the name
    let target = { type: "variable", name: name, line: line }
    let going = true
    while going {
        if pIsSym(".") {
            let dotTok = pCur()
            pAdvance()  // the '.'
            if pCur().kind != "name" {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "After '.' I need the name of a field, like: " + name + ".years. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
            }
            let fieldName = pCur().name
            pAdvance()
            target = { type: "field", base: target, name: fieldName, line: dotTok.line }
        } else {
            if pIsSym("[") {
                let bracketTok = pCur()
                pAdvance()  // the '['
                let idx = parseExpression()
                if idx.ok == false {
                    give idx
                }
                if pIsSym("]") == false {
                    give errAt(pCur().line, "I expected a ']' to close the '[' from line " + bracketTok.line + ", but I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
                }
                pAdvance()  // the ']'
                target = { type: "index", base: target, index: idx.value, line: bracketTok.line }
            } else {
                going = false
            }
        }
    }
    if pIsSym("=") == false {
        give errAt(pCur().line, "A line that starts with a field or an item like this needs to set it: write an '=' and the new value, like: " + name + ".years = 30 or " + name + "[0] = 30. To show it instead, write it after 'say'.")
    }
    pAdvance()  // the '='
    give okVal(target)
}

// The "( value, value )" part of a call. The current token is
// the '('.
make parseCallArgs(name) {
    pAdvance()  // the '('
    let args = []
    if pIsSym(")") == false {
        let collecting = true
        while collecting {
            let a = parseExpression()
            if a.ok == false {
                give a
            }
            append(args, a.value)
            if pIsSym(",") {
                pAdvance()
            } else {
                collecting = false
            }
        }
    }
    if pIsSym(")") == false {
        give errAt(pCur().line, "I expected a ')' to close the '(' in this call to '" + name + "', but I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
    }
    pAdvance()  // the ')'
    give okVal(args)
}

// ---------- expression parsing ----------

make parseExpression() {
    give parseEquality()
}

// One level of the precedence climb: parse a tighter level, then
// fold in any of this level's signs, left to right.
make parseBinaryLevel(tighter, ops) {
    let leftR = giveTighter(tighter)
    if leftR.ok == false {
        give leftR
    }
    let left = leftR.value
    let going = true
    while going {
        let t = pCur()
        if t.kind == "symbol" {
            if nameIndex(ops, t.symbol) >= 0 {
                pAdvance()
                let rightR = giveTighter(tighter)
                if rightR.ok == false {
                    give rightR
                }
                left = { type: "binary", op: t.symbol, left: left, right: rightR.value, line: t.line }
            } else {
                going = false
            }
        } else {
            going = false
        }
    }
    give okVal(left)
}

// Names the tighter level for parseBinaryLevel, so the four
// levels can share one body of code.
make giveTighter(which) {
    if which == "comparison" {
        give parseComparison()
    }
    if which == "sum" {
        give parseSum()
    }
    if which == "term" {
        give parseTerm()
    }
    give parseFactor()
}

make parseEquality() {
    give parseBinaryLevel("comparison", ["==", "!="])
}

make parseComparison() {
    give parseBinaryLevel("sum", ["<", "<=", ">", ">="])
}

make parseSum() {
    give parseBinaryLevel("term", ["+", "-"])
}

make parseTerm() {
    give parseBinaryLevel("factor", ["*", "/"])
}

make parseFactor() {
    if pIsSym("-") {
        let opTok = pCur()
        pAdvance()
        let inner = parseFactor()
        if inner.ok == false {
            give inner
        }
        give okVal({ type: "negate", inner: inner.value, line: opTok.line })
    }
    give parsePostfix()
}

// An atom followed by any number of steps: '.field' reads one
// field of an object, and '[...]' reads one item of a list or
// one character of some text.
make parsePostfix() {
    let exprR = parseAtom()
    if exprR.ok == false {
        give exprR
    }
    let expr = exprR.value
    let going = true
    while going {
        if pIsSym(".") {
            let dotTok = pCur()
            pAdvance()  // the '.'
            if pCur().kind != "name" {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "After '.' I need the name of a field, like: person.name. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
            }
            let fieldName = pCur().name
            pAdvance()
            expr = { type: "field", base: expr, name: fieldName, line: dotTok.line }
        } else {
            if pIsSym("[") {
                let bracketTok = pCur()
                pAdvance()  // the '['
                let idx = parseExpression()
                if idx.ok == false {
                    give idx
                }
                if pIsSym("]") == false {
                    give errAt(pCur().line, "I expected a ']' to close the '[' from line " + bracketTok.line + ", but I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
                }
                pAdvance()  // the ']'
                expr = { type: "index", base: expr, index: idx.value, line: bracketTok.line }
            } else {
                going = false
            }
        }
    }
    give okVal(expr)
}

make parseAtom() {
    let token = pCur()
    if token.kind == "number" {
        pAdvance()
        give okVal({ type: "number", value: token.num, line: token.line })
    }
    if token.kind == "text" {
        pAdvance()
        give okVal({ type: "text", value: token.text, line: token.line })
    }
    if token.kind == "keyword" {
        if token.word == "true" {
            pAdvance()
            give okVal({ type: "boolean", flag: true, line: token.line })
        }
        if token.word == "false" {
            pAdvance()
            give okVal({ type: "boolean", flag: false, line: token.line })
        }
        if token.word == "nothing" {
            pAdvance()
            give okVal({ type: "nothing", line: token.line })
        }
        if token.word == "floor" {
            give parseFloorExpr()
        }
    }
    if token.kind == "name" {
        pAdvance()
        if pIsSym("(") {
            let argsR = parseCallArgs(token.name)
            if argsR.ok == false {
                give argsR
            }
            give okVal({ type: "call", name: token.name, args: argsR.value, line: token.line })
        }
        give okVal({ type: "variable", name: token.name, line: token.line })
    }
    if token.kind == "symbol" {
        if token.symbol == "(" {
            pAdvance()
            let inner = parseExpression()
            if inner.ok == false {
                give inner
            }
            if pIsSym(")") == false {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "I expected a ')' to close the '(' from line " + token.line + ", but I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
            }
            pAdvance()
            give okVal(inner.value)
        }
        if token.symbol == "{" {
            give parseObjectLiteral(token)
        }
        if token.symbol == "[" {
            give parseListLiteral(token)
        }
    }
    if token.kind == "floor" {
        pAdvance()
        give okVal({ type: "floor", asm: token.asm, args: [], line: token.line })
    }
    give errAt(token.line, "I expected a number, a piece of text, true, false, a variable name, an object like { name: \"Tyler\" }, a list like [1, 2, 3], or a floor block here, but I found " + pSpoken(token) + ".")
}

// A list literal: '[' then values separated by commas, then ']'.
make parseListLiteral(openToken) {
    pAdvance()  // the '['
    let items = []
    if pIsSym("]") == false {
        let collecting = true
        while collecting {
            let item = parseExpression()
            if item.ok == false {
                give item
            }
            append(items, item.value)
            if pIsSym(",") {
                pAdvance()
            } else {
                collecting = false
            }
        }
    }
    if pIsSym("]") == false {
        give errAt(pCur().line, "I expected a ']' to close the list that started on line " + openToken.line + ", but I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
    }
    pAdvance()  // the ']'
    give okVal({ type: "list", items: items, line: openToken.line })
}

// An object literal: '{' then name ':' value pairs separated by
// commas, then '}'. A field named twice is refused here.
make parseObjectLiteral(openToken) {
    pAdvance()  // the '{'
    let names = []
    let values = []
    if pIsSym("}") == false {
        let collecting = true
        while collecting {
            if pCur().kind != "name" {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "Inside an object I need field names with values, like: { name: \"Tyler\", years: 29 }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
            }
            let fieldName = pCur().name
            let fieldLine = pCur().line
            pAdvance()
            if pIsSym(":") == false {
                give errAt(pCur().line, "After the field name '" + fieldName + "' I need a ':' and then its value, like: { " + fieldName + ": 29 }. Instead I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
            }
            pAdvance()  // the ':'
            if nameIndex(names, fieldName) >= 0 {
                give errAt(fieldLine, "This object names the field '" + fieldName + "' twice. Each field needs its own name — take one of the two out.")
            }
            let v = parseExpression()
            if v.ok == false {
                give v
            }
            append(names, fieldName)
            append(values, v.value)
            if pIsSym(",") {
                pAdvance()
            } else {
                collecting = false
            }
        }
    }
    if pIsSym("}") == false {
        give errAt(pCur().line, "I expected a '}' to close the object that started on line " + openToken.line + ", but I found " + pSpoken(pCur()) + ".")
    }
    pAdvance()  // the '}'
    give okVal({ type: "object", names: names, values: values, line: openToken.line })
}


// ---------- the token dump ----------

// ESC, as an explicit character loop: backslash first, then
// quote, newline, tab, carriage return; every other character
// passes through as it stands.
make escText(s) {
    let pieces = []
    for c in s {
        let piece = c
        if c == "\\" {
            piece = "\\\\"
        }
        if c == "\"" {
            piece = "\\\""
        }
        if c == "\n" {
            piece = "\\n"
        }
        if c == "\t" {
            piece = "\\t"
        }
        if c == CRCHAR {
            piece = "\\r"
        }
        append(pieces, piece)
    }
    give join(pieces, "")
}

make dumpToken(t) {
    if t.kind == "number" {
        give "number " + numText(t.num) + " @" + t.line
    }
    if t.kind == "text" {
        give "text \"" + escText(t.text) + "\" @" + t.line
    }
    if t.kind == "name" {
        give "name " + t.name + " @" + t.line
    }
    if t.kind == "keyword" {
        give "keyword " + t.word + " @" + t.line
    }
    if t.kind == "symbol" {
        give "symbol " + t.symbol + " @" + t.line
    }
    if t.kind == "floor" {
        give "floor \"" + escText(t.asm) + "\" @" + t.line
    }
    give "end @" + t.line
}

// ---------- the tree dump ----------
// The parse tree as ONE line, in the contract's format —
// the oracle (tools/front-oracle.swift) prints exactly this
// from the Swift tree, and run-front-parser.sh holds the two
// byte for byte. <KIND> is 'none' when there is no
// annotation, otherwise the kind string in double quotes;
// <ESC> is the token dump's escaping.
//
//   Program: (program + for each statement " " + stmt + ")"
//     — an empty program is (program)
//   (number <value> @L)   (text "<ESC>" @L)
//   (boolean true @L) / (boolean false @L)   (nothing @L)
//   (variable <name> @L)
//   (call <name> @L + args + ")"
//   (object @L + for each field " (<name> " + expr + ")" + ")"
//   (list @L + items + ")"
//   (field <base> <name> @L)   (index <base> <idx> @L)
//   (floor "<ESC>" @L + args + ")"
//   (negate <expr> @L)   (binary <op> @L <left> <right>)
//   (declare <name> <KIND> <expr> @L)   (assign <name> <expr> @L)
//   (assign-target <target> <expr> @L)
//   (say <expr> @L)   (give <expr> @L)
//   (if <cond> @L (then + stmts + ") (else" + absent: " none",
//     present: stmts + "))" — a present-but-empty else is (else)
//   (while <cond> @L + body + ")"   (repeat <count> @L + body + ")"
//   (for <name> <over> @L + body + ")"
//   (define <name> @L (params + for each " (<pname> <KIND>)"
//     + ") (returns <KIND>)" + body + ")"
//   (attempt @L (body + stmts + ") (recovery" + stmts + "))"
//   (do-call <name> @L + args + ")"   (do-expr <expr> @L)
//
// Each list of fragments is gathered in a list and joined
// once, the oracle's way — the OS sources' trees run to
// megabytes on the one line, and joining text onto text in
// a loop would crawl.

make kindText(kindWord) {
    if kindWord == "" {
        give "none"
    }
    give "\"" + kindWord + "\""
}

// " stmt stmt ..." — the fragments of a block, joined once.
make dumpStmtsText(stmts) {
    let parts = []
    for s in stmts {
        append(parts, " ")
        append(parts, dumpStmt(s))
    }
    give join(parts, "")
}

// " expr expr ..." — the fragments of a call's values.
make dumpArgsText(args) {
    let parts = []
    for a in args {
        append(parts, " ")
        append(parts, dumpExpr(a))
    }
    give join(parts, "")
}

make dumpExpr(e) {
    if e.type == "number" {
        give "(number " + numText(e.value) + " @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "text" {
        give "(text \"" + escText(e.value) + "\" @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "boolean" {
        if e.flag {
            give "(boolean true @" + e.line + ")"
        }
        give "(boolean false @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "nothing" {
        give "(nothing @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "variable" {
        give "(variable " + e.name + " @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "call" {
        give "(call " + e.name + " @" + e.line + dumpArgsText(e.args) + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "object" {
        let parts = ["(object @" + e.line]
        let i = 0
        while i < length(e.names) {
            append(parts, " (" + e.names[i] + " ")
            append(parts, dumpExpr(e.values[i]))
            append(parts, ")")
            i = i + 1
        }
        append(parts, ")")
        give join(parts, "")
    }
    if e.type == "list" {
        let parts = ["(list @" + e.line]
        for item in e.items {
            append(parts, " ")
            append(parts, dumpExpr(item))
        }
        append(parts, ")")
        give join(parts, "")
    }
    if e.type == "field" {
        give "(field " + dumpExpr(e.base) + " " + e.name + " @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "index" {
        give "(index " + dumpExpr(e.base) + " " + dumpExpr(e.index) + " @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "floor" {
        give "(floor \"" + escText(e.asm) + "\" @" + e.line + dumpArgsText(e.args) + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "negate" {
        give "(negate " + dumpExpr(e.inner) + " @" + e.line + ")"
    }
    if e.type == "binary" {
        give "(binary " + e.op + " @" + e.line + " " + dumpExpr(e.left) + " " + dumpExpr(e.right) + ")"
    }
    give "(unknown)"
}

make dumpStmt(s) {
    if s.type == "declare" {
        give "(declare " + s.name + " " + kindText(s.kindWord) + " " + dumpExpr(s.value) + " @" + s.line + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "assign" {
        give "(assign " + s.name + " " + dumpExpr(s.value) + " @" + s.line + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "assignTarget" {
        give "(assign-target " + dumpExpr(s.target) + " " + dumpExpr(s.value) + " @" + s.line + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "say" {
        give "(say " + dumpExpr(s.value) + " @" + s.line + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "give" {
        give "(give " + dumpExpr(s.value) + " @" + s.line + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "if" {
        let elseText = " none"
        if s.hasElse {
            elseText = dumpStmtsText(s.elseStmts)
        }
        give "(if " + dumpExpr(s.condition) + " @" + s.line + " (then" + dumpStmtsText(s.thenStmts) + ") (else" + elseText + "))"
    }
    if s.type == "while" {
        give "(while " + dumpExpr(s.condition) + " @" + s.line + dumpStmtsText(s.body) + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "repeat" {
        give "(repeat " + dumpExpr(s.count) + " @" + s.line + dumpStmtsText(s.body) + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "for" {
        give "(for " + s.name + " " + dumpExpr(s.over) + " @" + s.line + dumpStmtsText(s.body) + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "define" {
        let parts = ["(define " + s.name + " @" + s.line + " (params"]
        let i = 0
        while i < length(s.params) {
            append(parts, " (" + s.params[i] + " " + kindText(s.paramKinds[i]) + ")")
            i = i + 1
        }
        append(parts, ") (returns " + kindText(s.returnKind) + ")")
        append(parts, dumpStmtsText(s.body))
        append(parts, ")")
        give join(parts, "")
    }
    if s.type == "attempt" {
        give "(attempt @" + s.line + " (body" + dumpStmtsText(s.body) + ") (recovery" + dumpStmtsText(s.recovery) + "))"
    }
    if s.type == "doCall" {
        give "(do-call " + s.name + " @" + s.line + dumpArgsText(s.args) + ")"
    }
    if s.type == "doExpr" {
        give "(do-expr " + dumpExpr(s.expr) + " @" + s.line + ")"
    }
    give "(unknown)"
}

make dumpProgram(stmts) {
    give "(program" + dumpStmtsText(stmts) + ")"
}

// ---------- the checker ----------
// Stone 50: the checker begins. The Swift checker
// (src/Checker.swift) ported into Nifla, fresh — a
// SUPERSESSION, not a convergence (the decision and its
// reason are recorded in the README ledger for this
// stone): the self-hosted interpreter has no static
// checker to lift — it runs no pre-pass, and its guards
// are runtime guards in the interpreter's own words — so
// this section is written against Checker.swift as the
// sole standard, reusing the front end's existing helpers
// (errAt/okVal result objects, nameIndex, numText) and the
// self-host's kind-word helpers' wording where they mirror
// the checker's phrases. The self-host stays untouched.
//
// The walk mirrors Swift's, refusal for refusal: it walks
// the parser's tagged-object trees in the order the
// program would run, and the FIRST refusal in walk order
// is the verdict, handed back as an errAt result. Kinds
// are carried as text — "number", "text", "boolean",
// "object", "list", "bytes", "unknown", "nothing", and
// "maybe <inner>" for a maybe kind — and a kind word that
// names no kind (Swift's nil from fromKindWord, which is
// what the word "nothing" maps to there) is carried as "".
//
// In this stone the walk is the HOSTED walk only:
// check-bare and check-guest run this same walk, and
// chkBareTool below answers "not a bare tool" for every
// name — the bare/guest tool doors and the bare floor-text
// rule land in Stone 51, which extends chkBareTool in this
// one place and teaches chkFloor its bare branches.
//
// A writing note, learned the hard way in this stone:
// several tables and walks below are written in
// assignment style (a result variable set inside 'if'
// blocks, given once at the end) rather than the
// guard-and-give style the rest of the file uses. The
// compiled door's generated Swift warns once for every
// block whose scope nothing uses (a block holding only a
// 'give' of a constant is the common case), and niflac
// reads its Swift compiler's error channel only after
// the compiler exits — past the pipe's 64 KB the two
// deadlock. The front end's diagnostics stood at 35 KB
// before this stone; a first guard-style draft of this
// section took them to 132 KB and no compile ever
// finished. Assignment style keeps the whole file at
// 44 KB. Keep new checker code in this style, and if a
// future stone's compile ever hangs with the compiler
// idle, count the warnings first.

// Whether a kind is a maybe kind, and its inner kind.
make chkIsMaybe(kind) {
    if length(kind) > 6 {
        give part(kind, 0, 6) == "maybe "
    }
    give false
}

make chkMaybeInner(kind) {
    give part(kind, 6, length(kind) - 6)
}

// How a kind is named in a plain-words message —
// StaticKind.phrase in Checker.swift.
make chkPhrase(kind) {
    let out = "a value"
    if kind == "number" {
        out = "a number"
    }
    if kind == "text" {
        out = "some text"
    }
    if kind == "boolean" {
        out = "a true/false value"
    }
    if kind == "object" {
        out = "an object"
    }
    if kind == "list" {
        out = "a list"
    }
    if kind == "bytes" {
        out = "a block of bytes"
    }
    if kind == "nothing" {
        out = "nothing"
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(kind) {
        out = chkPhrase(chkMaybeInner(kind)) + " or nothing"
    }
    give out
}

// The kind an annotation word names, as kind text —
// StaticKind.fromKindWord; "" stands for Swift's nil
// (the word "nothing", and any word that names no kind).
make chkFromWord(word) {
    let out = ""
    if word == "number" {
        out = "number"
    }
    if word == "text" {
        out = "text"
    }
    if word == "boolean" {
        out = "boolean"
    }
    if word == "list" {
        out = "list"
    }
    if word == "object" {
        out = "object"
    }
    if word == "bytes" {
        out = "bytes"
    }
    if length(word) > 6 {
        if part(word, 0, 6) == "maybe " {
            let inner = chkFromWord(part(word, 6, length(word) - 6))
            if inner == "" {
                out = ""
            } else {
                out = "maybe " + inner
            }
        }
    }
    give out
}

// How an annotation word itself is phrased —
// StaticKind.phrase(forKindWord:).
make chkPhraseForWord(word) {
    let kind = chkFromWord(word)
    if kind != "" {
        give chkPhrase(kind)
    }
    give "nothing"
}

// How two proven kinds join where the program could have
// gone either way — StaticKind.mergedKind, case for case.
make chkMergedKind(a, b) {
    if a == b {
        give a
    }
    if a == "nothing" {
        if chkIsMaybe(b) {
            give b
        }
        if b != "unknown" {
            give "maybe " + b
        }
        give "unknown"
    }
    if b == "nothing" {
        if chkIsMaybe(a) {
            give a
        }
        if a != "unknown" {
            give "maybe " + a
        }
        give "unknown"
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(a) {
        if chkMaybeInner(a) == b {
            give a
        }
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(b) {
        if chkMaybeInner(b) == a {
            give b
        }
    }
    give "unknown"
}

// Edit distance between two words, for "Did you mean"
// suggestions — the self-host's copy of the Swift
// interpreter's measure, carried into the front end for
// the checker (the front end's lexer and parser never
// needed it).
make chkEditDistance(a, b) {
    let ac = []
    for c in a {
        append(ac, c)
    }
    let bc = []
    for c in b {
        append(bc, c)
    }
    if length(ac) == 0 {
        give length(bc)
    }
    if length(bc) == 0 {
        give length(ac)
    }
    let prev = []
    let i0 = 0
    while i0 <= length(bc) {
        append(prev, i0)
        i0 = i0 + 1
    }
    let i = 1
    while i <= length(ac) {
        let cur = [i]
        let j = 1
        while j <= length(bc) {
            let cost = 1
            if ac[i - 1] == bc[j - 1] {
                cost = 0
            }
            let best = prev[j] + 1
            if cur[j - 1] + 1 < best {
                best = cur[j - 1] + 1
            }
            if prev[j - 1] + cost < best {
                best = prev[j - 1] + cost
            }
            append(cur, best)
            j = j + 1
        }
        prev = cur
        i = i + 1
    }
    give prev[length(bc)]
}

// ---------- name maps ----------
// A name map is an object with parallel lists —
// { names: [...], vals: [...] } — because a Nifla object
// can't build a field name from text (the front end's
// nameIndex pattern, the self-host's shape). Absence from
// the map is how a missing fact (Swift's nil) is carried:
// no sentinel value is ever stored.

make nmNew() {
    give { names: [], vals: [] }
}

make nmHas(map, name) {
    give nameIndex(map.names, name) >= 0
}

make nmGet(map, name) {
    let i = nameIndex(map.names, name)
    if i >= 0 {
        give { found: true, value: map.vals[i] }
    }
    give { found: false, value: "" }
}

make nmSet(map, name, value) {
    let i = nameIndex(map.names, name)
    if i >= 0 {
        map.vals[i] = value
    } else {
        append(map.names, name)
        append(map.vals, value)
    }
}

make nmRemove(map, name) {
    let i = nameIndex(map.names, name)
    if i >= 0 {
        removeat(map.names, i)
        removeat(map.vals, i)
    }
}

make nmClear(map) {
    while length(map.names) > 0 {
        removeat(map.names, 0)
        removeat(map.vals, 0)
    }
}

make nmCopy(map) {
    let out = nmNew()
    let i = 0
    while i < length(map.names) {
        append(out.names, map.names[i])
        append(out.vals, map.vals[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    give out
}

// The names of two maps joined, each name once, the first
// map's order then the second's new names.
make nmUnionNames(a, b) {
    let out = []
    for n in a.names {
        append(out, n)
    }
    for n in b.names {
        if nameIndex(out, n) < 0 {
            append(out, n)
        }
    }
    give out
}

// ---------- the static environment ----------
// StaticEnv's twin: an object { scopes, annotations,
// constants, emptyLists, lengths }, each a list of name
// maps, one map per scope. Swift's StaticEnv is a value —
// a copy is a snapshot — so where Swift copies, this port
// deep-copies (envCopy) and where Swift reassigns its
// variable, this port writes the new picture into the
// same object (envBecome), because a Nifla function's
// caller holds the object itself, not the variable.

make envNew() {
    give { scopes: [nmNew()], annotations: [nmNew()], constants: [nmNew()], emptyLists: [nmNew()], lengths: [nmNew()] }
}

make envCopyMaps(maps) {
    let out = []
    for m in maps {
        append(out, nmCopy(m))
    }
    give out
}

make envCopy(e) {
    give { scopes: envCopyMaps(e.scopes), annotations: envCopyMaps(e.annotations), constants: envCopyMaps(e.constants), emptyLists: envCopyMaps(e.emptyLists), lengths: envCopyMaps(e.lengths) }
}

make envBecome(e, other) {
    let c = envCopy(other)
    e.scopes = c.scopes
    e.annotations = c.annotations
    e.constants = c.constants
    e.emptyLists = c.emptyLists
    e.lengths = c.lengths
}

// A lookup down one of the env's pictures, scopes from the
// innermost out — { found, value }, absence for nil.
make envLookupIn(maps, name) {
    let i = length(maps) - 1
    while i >= 0 {
        let g = nmGet(maps[i], name)
        if g.found {
            give g
        }
        i = i - 1
    }
    give { found: false, value: "" }
}

make envLookup(e, name) {
    give envLookupIn(e.scopes, name)
}

make envLookupAnn(e, name) {
    give envLookupIn(e.annotations, name)
}

make envLookupConst(e, name) {
    give envLookupIn(e.constants, name)
}

make envLookupLength(e, name) {
    give envLookupIn(e.lengths, name)
}

make envIsProvenEmpty(e, name) {
    let g = envLookupIn(e.emptyLists, name)
    if g.found {
        give g.value
    }
    give false
}

make envIsDeclaredHere(e, name) {
    give nmHas(e.scopes[length(e.scopes) - 1], name)
}

// A fact bundle for declare/assign: { found, value } for a
// tracked constant or length, carried the same way.
make envDeclare(e, name, kind, annWord, constObj, provenEmpty, lenObj) {
    let top = length(e.scopes) - 1
    nmSet(e.scopes[top], name, kind)
    if annWord != "" {
        nmSet(e.annotations[top], name, annWord)
    }
    if constObj.found {
        nmSet(e.constants[top], name, constObj.value)
    }
    if provenEmpty {
        nmSet(e.emptyLists[top], name, true)
    }
    if lenObj.found {
        nmSet(e.lengths[top], name, lenObj.value)
    }
}

// Sets the kind in whichever block made the name; true
// when some block knows the name, as Swift's assign.
make envAssign(e, name, kind, constObj, provenEmpty, lenObj) {
    let i = length(e.scopes) - 1
    while i >= 0 {
        if nmHas(e.scopes[i], name) {
            nmSet(e.scopes[i], name, kind)
            if constObj.found {
                nmSet(e.constants[i], name, constObj.value)
            } else {
                nmRemove(e.constants[i], name)
            }
            if provenEmpty {
                nmSet(e.emptyLists[i], name, true)
            } else {
                nmRemove(e.emptyLists[i], name)
            }
            if lenObj.found {
                nmSet(e.lengths[i], name, lenObj.value)
            } else {
                nmRemove(e.lengths[i], name)
            }
            give true
        }
        i = i - 1
    }
    give false
}

// Sets a name's kind where it was made and lets its
// tracked facts go — the narrowing a check against
// 'nothing' earns.
make envNarrow(e, name, kind) {
    let i = length(e.scopes) - 1
    let done = false
    while done == false {
        if i < 0 {
            done = true
        }
        if done == false {
            if nmHas(e.scopes[i], name) {
                nmSet(e.scopes[i], name, kind)
                nmRemove(e.constants[i], name)
                nmRemove(e.emptyLists[i], name)
                nmRemove(e.lengths[i], name)
                done = true
            }
            i = i - 1
        }
    }
}

// Takes away every tracked fact in every scope, keeping
// kinds and annotations (a function body is checked with
// only the facts the body itself establishes).
make envDropFacts(e) {
    for m in e.constants {
        nmClear(m)
    }
    for m in e.emptyLists {
        nmClear(m)
    }
    for m in e.lengths {
        nmClear(m)
    }
}

// Takes away the facts (not the kind, not the annotation)
// for one name.
make envForgetFacts(e, name) {
    let i = length(e.scopes) - 1
    let done = false
    while done == false {
        if i < 0 {
            done = true
        }
        if done == false {
            if nmHas(e.scopes[i], name) {
                nmRemove(e.constants[i], name)
                nmRemove(e.emptyLists[i], name)
                nmRemove(e.lengths[i], name)
                done = true
            }
            i = i - 1
        }
    }
}

make envAllNames(e) {
    let out = []
    for scope in e.scopes {
        for n in scope.names {
            append(out, n)
        }
    }
    give out
}

make envPush(e) {
    append(e.scopes, nmNew())
    append(e.annotations, nmNew())
    append(e.constants, nmNew())
    append(e.emptyLists, nmNew())
    append(e.lengths, nmNew())
}

make envPop(e) {
    removeat(e.scopes, length(e.scopes) - 1)
    removeat(e.annotations, length(e.annotations) - 1)
    removeat(e.constants, length(e.constants) - 1)
    removeat(e.emptyLists, length(e.emptyLists) - 1)
    removeat(e.lengths, length(e.lengths) - 1)
}

// After a function call, forget every proven kind and
// every fact; names and annotations stay.
make envForgetKinds(e) {
    for scope in e.scopes {
        let i = 0
        while i < length(scope.names) {
            scope.vals[i] = "unknown"
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    for m in e.constants {
        nmClear(m)
    }
    for m in e.emptyLists {
        nmClear(m)
    }
    for m in e.lengths {
        nmClear(m)
    }
}

// Merge two ways the program could have gone, exactly as
// StaticEnv.merged: the result starts from a, and its
// scopes 0..<min(count) are rewritten name by name —
// scopes beyond the shorter count are a's, kept whole.
make envMerged(a, b) {
    let result = envCopy(a)
    let count = length(a.scopes)
    if length(b.scopes) < count {
        count = length(b.scopes)
    }
    let idx = 0
    while idx < count {
        let scope = nmNew()
        let names = nmUnionNames(a.scopes[idx], b.scopes[idx])
        for name in names {
            let ga = nmGet(a.scopes[idx], name)
            let gb = nmGet(b.scopes[idx], name)
            if ga.found {
                if gb.found {
                    nmSet(scope, name, chkMergedKind(ga.value, gb.value))
                } else {
                    nmSet(scope, name, ga.value)
                }
            } else {
                nmSet(scope, name, gb.value)
            }
        }
        result.scopes[idx] = scope
        let anns = nmNew()
        let annNames = nmUnionNames(a.annotations[idx], b.annotations[idx])
        for name in annNames {
            let ga2 = nmGet(a.annotations[idx], name)
            if ga2.found {
                nmSet(anns, name, ga2.value)
            } else {
                let gb2 = nmGet(b.annotations[idx], name)
                nmSet(anns, name, gb2.value)
            }
        }
        result.annotations[idx] = anns
        let consts = nmNew()
        let constNames = nmUnionNames(a.constants[idx], b.constants[idx])
        for name in constNames {
            let ga3 = nmGet(a.constants[idx], name)
            let gb3 = nmGet(b.constants[idx], name)
            if ga3.found {
                if gb3.found {
                    if ga3.value == gb3.value {
                        nmSet(consts, name, ga3.value)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        result.constants[idx] = consts
        let empties = nmNew()
        let emptyNames = nmUnionNames(a.emptyLists[idx], b.emptyLists[idx])
        for name in emptyNames {
            let ga4 = nmGet(a.emptyLists[idx], name)
            let gb4 = nmGet(b.emptyLists[idx], name)
            if ga4.found {
                if gb4.found {
                    if ga4.value == true {
                        if gb4.value == true {
                            nmSet(empties, name, true)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        result.emptyLists[idx] = empties
        let lens = nmNew()
        let lenNames = nmUnionNames(a.lengths[idx], b.lengths[idx])
        for name in lenNames {
            let ga5 = nmGet(a.lengths[idx], name)
            let gb5 = nmGet(b.lengths[idx], name)
            if ga5.found {
                if gb5.found {
                    if ga5.value == gb5.value {
                        nmSet(lens, name, ga5.value)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        result.lengths[idx] = lens
        idx = idx + 1
    }
    give result
}

// ---------- the built-in tools, as the checker sees them ----------
// The checker's rules for the built-in tools: the kind a
// call provably gives back, and for each value the tool
// takes, the kinds that value may have and how the need is
// phrased — builtinRules in Checker.swift, transcribed
// exactly, needs-phrases included. A needs entry with
// hasAllowed false is Swift's nil kind set: any kind will
// do. The parameter names and the example come from
// builtinTools in src/Interpreter.swift — only the COUNT
// of parameters is used for checking, and the names and
// example only for the count-mismatch message
// (builtinCountMessage there, mirrored below).

make chkNeed(allowed, need) {
    give { hasAllowed: true, allowed: allowed, need: need }
}

make chkNeedAny() {
    give { hasAllowed: false, allowed: [], need: "" }
}

make chkRule(result, needs) {
    give { found: true, result: result, needs: needs }
}

make chkBuiltinRule(name) {
    let result = { found: false, result: "unknown", needs: [] }
    if name == "length" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["text", "list", "object", "bytes"], "some text, a list, an object, or a block of bytes to measure")])
    }
    if name == "join" {
        result = chkRule("text", [chkNeed(["list"], "a list to join"), chkNeed(["text"], "some text to join the items with")])
    }
    if name == "split" {
        result = chkRule("list", [chkNeed(["text"], "some text to split"), chkNeed(["text"], "some text to split by")])
    }
    if name == "part" {
        result = chkRule("text", [chkNeed(["text"], "some text to take a part of"), chkNeed(["number"], "a number saying which character to start at"), chkNeed(["number"], "a number saying how many characters to take")])
    }
    if name == "find" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["text"], "some text to look in"), chkNeed(["text"], "some text to look for")])
    }
    if name == "append" {
        result = chkRule("list", [chkNeed(["list"], "a list to add to"), chkNeedAny()])
    }
    if name == "readfile" {
        result = chkRule("text", [chkNeed(["text"], "the name of a file, as text")])
    }
    if name == "readbytes" {
        result = chkRule("bytes", [chkNeed(["text"], "the name of a file, as text")])
    }
    if name == "writefile" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["text"], "the name of a file, as text"), chkNeed(["text"], "some text to write")])
    }
    if name == "arguments" {
        result = chkRule("list", [])
    }
    if name == "makebytes" {
        result = chkRule("bytes", [chkNeed(["number"], "a number saying how many bytes to make")])
    }
    if name == "sayerr" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeedAny()])
    }
    if name == "exitwith" {
        result = chkRule("unknown", [chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number from 0 to 255 to end with")])
    }
    if name == "removeat" {
        result = chkRule("unknown", [chkNeed(["list"], "a list to take an item from"), chkNeed(["number"], "a number saying which item to take out")])
    }
    if name == "insertat" {
        result = chkRule("list", [chkNeed(["list"], "a list to put an item into"), chkNeed(["number"], "a number saying where to put the new item"), chkNeedAny()])
    }
    if name == "sorted" {
        result = chkRule("list", [chkNeed(["list"], "a list to sort")])
    }
    if name == "fieldnames" {
        result = chkRule("list", [chkNeed(["object"], "an object to list the field names of")])
    }
    if name == "removefield" {
        result = chkRule("unknown", [chkNeed(["object"], "an object to take a field from"), chkNeed(["text"], "the name of a field, as text")])
    }
    if name == "asnumber" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["text"], "some text to read a number from")])
    }
    if name == "bitand" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to work on"), chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to work on")])
    }
    if name == "bitor" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to work on"), chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to work on")])
    }
    if name == "bitxor" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to work on"), chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to work on")])
    }
    if name == "astext" {
        result = chkRule("text", [chkNeed(["bytes"], "a block of bytes to read as text")])
    }
    if name == "wholediv" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to divide"), chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to divide by")])
    }
    if name == "leftover" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to divide"), chkNeed(["number"], "a whole number to divide by")])
    }
    if name == "rounddown" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a number to round")])
    }
    if name == "roundup" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a number to round")])
    }
    if name == "absolute" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a number to take the size of")])
    }
    if name == "smallest" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a number to choose between"), chkNeed(["number"], "a number to choose between")])
    }
    if name == "largest" {
        result = chkRule("number", [chkNeed(["number"], "a number to choose between"), chkNeed(["number"], "a number to choose between")])
    }
    give result
}

make chkBuiltinDef(name) {
    let result = { found: false, params: [], example: "" }
    if name == "length" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["value"], example: "length(\"Tyler\")" }
    }
    if name == "join" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["list", "separator"], example: "join(words, \" \")" }
    }
    if name == "split" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["text", "separator"], example: "split(\"a,b\", \",\")" }
    }
    if name == "part" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["text", "start", "count"], example: "part(\"Tyler\", 0, 2)" }
    }
    if name == "find" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["text", "piece"], example: "find(\"Tyler\", \"y\")" }
    }
    if name == "append" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["list", "value"], example: "append(items, 4)" }
    }
    if name == "readfile" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["path"], example: "readfile(\"notes.txt\")" }
    }
    if name == "readbytes" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["path"], example: "readbytes(\"notes.txt\")" }
    }
    if name == "writefile" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["path", "text"], example: "writefile(\"notes.txt\", \"Shalom\")" }
    }
    if name == "arguments" {
        result = { found: true, params: [], example: "arguments()" }
    }
    if name == "makebytes" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["count"], example: "makebytes(8)" }
    }
    if name == "sayerr" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["value"], example: "sayerr(\"Mind the step.\")" }
    }
    if name == "exitwith" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["code"], example: "exitwith(1)" }
    }
    if name == "removeat" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["list", "index"], example: "removeat(items, 0)" }
    }
    if name == "insertat" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["list", "index", "value"], example: "insertat(items, 0, 4)" }
    }
    if name == "sorted" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["list"], example: "sorted(items)" }
    }
    if name == "fieldnames" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["object"], example: "fieldnames(person)" }
    }
    if name == "removefield" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["object", "name"], example: "removefield(person, \"town\")" }
    }
    if name == "asnumber" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["text"], example: "asnumber(\"620\")" }
    }
    if name == "bitand" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["first", "second"], example: "bitand(12, 10)" }
    }
    if name == "bitor" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["first", "second"], example: "bitor(12, 10)" }
    }
    if name == "bitxor" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["first", "second"], example: "bitxor(12, 10)" }
    }
    if name == "astext" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["bytes"], example: "astext(block)" }
    }
    if name == "wholediv" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["first", "second"], example: "wholediv(17, 5)" }
    }
    if name == "leftover" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["first", "second"], example: "leftover(17, 5)" }
    }
    if name == "rounddown" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["value"], example: "rounddown(3.5)" }
    }
    if name == "roundup" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["value"], example: "roundup(3.5)" }
    }
    if name == "absolute" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["value"], example: "absolute(-7)" }
    }
    if name == "smallest" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["first", "second"], example: "smallest(3, 7)" }
    }
    if name == "largest" {
        result = { found: true, params: ["first", "second"], example: "largest(3, 7)" }
    }
    give result
}

// "no values", "1 value (name)", "2 values (a, b)" — the
// words both count messages use.
make chkTakesText(params) {
    if length(params) == 0 {
        give "no values"
    }
    if length(params) == 1 {
        give "1 value (" + params[0] + ")"
    }
    give "" + length(params) + " values (" + join(params, ", ") + ")"
}

// builtinCountMessage in src/Interpreter.swift, word for
// word.
make chkBuiltinCountMessage(name, def, given) {
    give "The tool '" + name + "' takes " + chkTakesText(def.params) + ", but it was given " + given + ". Call it like: " + def.example + "."
}

// ---------- the functions the program makes ----------
// Collected before checking, in a name map: each record is
// { params, paramKinds, returnKind }, paramKinds a list of
// kind words ("" for none) and returnKind a kind word or
// "". The FIRST define of a name wins, as in Swift.

let gChkFuncs = { names: [], vals: [] }
let gChkReturnKind = ""
let gChkFuncName = ""

make chkCollectFunctions(stmts) {
    for s in stmts {
        if s.type == "define" {
            if nmHas(gChkFuncs, s.name) == false {
                nmSet(gChkFuncs, s.name, { params: s.params, paramKinds: s.paramKinds, returnKind: s.returnKind })
            }
            chkCollectFunctions(s.body)
        }
        if s.type == "attempt" {
            chkCollectFunctions(s.body)
            chkCollectFunctions(s.recovery)
        }
        if s.type == "if" {
            chkCollectFunctions(s.thenStmts)
            if s.hasElse {
                chkCollectFunctions(s.elseStmts)
            }
        }
        if s.type == "while" {
            chkCollectFunctions(s.body)
        }
        if s.type == "repeat" {
            chkCollectFunctions(s.body)
        }
        if s.type == "for" {
            chkCollectFunctions(s.body)
        }
    }
}

// The names 'let' makes directly in a block.
make chkDeclaredNames(stmts) {
    let out = []
    for s in stmts {
        if s.type == "declare" {
            append(out, s.name)
        }
    }
    give out
}

make chkUnionNames(a, b) {
    let out = []
    for n in a {
        append(out, n)
    }
    for n in b {
        if nameIndex(out, n) < 0 {
            append(out, n)
        }
    }
    give out
}

make chkContainsName(names, name) {
    give nameIndex(names, name) >= 0
}

// The closest made function's name, over the SORTED
// candidate list, first strictly-smallest distance of at
// most 2 winning — as Swift's closestFunctionName.
make chkClosestFunction(name) {
    let candidates = sorted(gChkFuncs.names)
    let bestName = ""
    let bestDist = 0
    let hasBest = false
    for candidate in candidates {
        if candidate != name {
            let d = chkEditDistance(name, candidate)
            if d <= 2 {
                if hasBest == false {
                    bestName = candidate
                    bestDist = d
                    hasBest = true
                } else {
                    if d < bestDist {
                        bestName = candidate
                        bestDist = d
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if hasBest {
        give { found: true, name: bestName }
    }
    give { found: false, name: "" }
}

// The closest name in an environment, the same shape —
// Swift's closestName, over the sorted set of all names.
make chkClosestName(name, e) {
    let candidates = sorted(envAllNames(e))
    let bestName = ""
    let bestDist = 0
    let hasBest = false
    for candidate in candidates {
        if candidate != name {
            let d = chkEditDistance(name, candidate)
            if d <= 2 {
                if hasBest == false {
                    bestName = candidate
                    bestDist = d
                    hasBest = true
                } else {
                    if d < bestDist {
                        bestName = candidate
                        bestDist = d
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if hasBest {
        give { found: true, name: bestName }
    }
    give { found: false, name: "" }
}

// ---------- annotations and provable facts (Stone 18/19 helpers) ----------

make chkAnnotationSatisfied(proven, wanted) {
    let out = false
    if proven == wanted {
        out = true
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(wanted) {
        if proven == chkMaybeInner(wanted) {
            out = true
        }
        if proven == "nothing" {
            out = true
        }
    }
    give out
}

// The kind a name is recorded under — declaredKind.
make chkDeclaredKind(kindWord, proven) {
    let out = proven
    let decided = false
    if kindWord == "" {
        decided = true
    }
    if decided == false {
        if kindWord == "nothing" {
            out = "nothing"
            decided = true
        }
    }
    if decided == false {
        let wanted = chkFromWord(kindWord)
        if wanted == "" {
            out = "unknown"
        } else {
            out = wanted
            if proven != "unknown" {
                if chkAnnotationSatisfied(proven, wanted) {
                    out = proven
                }
            }
        }
    }
    give out
}

// The refusal for a use of a value that may be nothing,
// where a value that is surely there is needed.
make chkUnhandledMaybe(kind, line) {
    give errAt(line, "This uses " + chkPhrase(kind) + " — a value that may be nothing — where a value that is surely there is needed, and nothing is the empty value: there is nothing there to use. Check the value first, like: if value == nothing { ... } else { ... } — in the 'else', the value is known to be there. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
}

// Refuses when a proven kind can never satisfy an
// annotation; silent when the kind can't be proven.
make chkCheckAnnotation(kindWord, proven, line, wording) {
    if kindWord == "nothing" {
        if proven != "unknown" {
            if proven != "nothing" {
                give errAt(line, wording + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    let wanted = chkFromWord(kindWord)
    if wanted == "" {
        give okVal("")
    }
    if proven != "unknown" {
        if chkAnnotationSatisfied(proven, wanted) == false {
            give errAt(line, wording + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
        }
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// The annotation check at a call.
make chkCheckCallAnnotations(name, record, argKinds, line) {
    let i = 0
    while i < length(record.paramKinds) {
        let kindWord = record.paramKinds[i]
        if kindWord != "" {
            if i < length(argKinds) {
                let proven = argKinds[i]
                if proven != "unknown" {
                    let r = chkCheckAnnotation(kindWord, proven, line, "The value handed to '" + name + "' for '" + record.params[i] + "' is " + chkPhrase(proven) + ", but '" + record.params[i] + "' is marked as " + chkPhraseForWord(kindWord) + " by its annotation. Change what the call hands over, or change the annotation.")
                    if r.ok == false {
                        give r
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// The annotation check at a 'give'.
make chkCheckGive(kind, line) {
    if gChkReturnKind == "" {
        give okVal("")
    }
    give chkCheckAnnotation(gChkReturnKind, kind, line, "'" + gChkFuncName + "' is marked to give back " + chkPhraseForWord(gChkReturnKind) + " by its annotation, but this 'give' hands back " + chkPhrase(kind) + ". Change the value, or change the annotation.")
}

// The number an expression provably holds — { found,
// value } — from literals and tracked facts only.
make chkConstNumber(expr, e) {
    if expr.type == "number" {
        give { found: true, value: expr.value }
    }
    if expr.type == "negate" {
        let inner = chkConstNumber(expr.inner, e)
        if inner.found {
            give { found: true, value: 0 - inner.value }
        }
        give { found: false, value: 0 }
    }
    if expr.type == "variable" {
        let g = envLookupConst(e, expr.name)
        if g.found {
            give { found: true, value: g.value }
        }
        give { found: false, value: 0 }
    }
    if expr.type == "binary" {
        let a = chkConstNumber(expr.left, e)
        let b = chkConstNumber(expr.right, e)
        if a.found {
            if b.found {
                if expr.op == "+" {
                    give { found: true, value: a.value + b.value }
                }
                if expr.op == "-" {
                    give { found: true, value: a.value - b.value }
                }
                if expr.op == "*" {
                    give { found: true, value: a.value * b.value }
                }
                if expr.op == "/" {
                    if b.value == 0 {
                        give { found: false, value: 0 }
                    }
                    give { found: true, value: a.value / b.value }
                }
            }
        }
        give { found: false, value: 0 }
    }
    give { found: false, value: 0 }
}

// A tracked constant read as a whole number, when it is
// one and its size is honest — Swift's Int conversion in
// the range checks.
make chkWholeOf(constObj) {
    if constObj.found == false {
        give { found: false, value: 0 }
    }
    let v = constObj.value
    if v == rounddown(v) {
        if absolute(v) < 1000000000000000 {
            give { found: true, value: v }
        }
    }
    give { found: false, value: 0 }
}

// The length an expression provably holds, when it is a
// literal list or text — { found, value }.
make chkLiteralLength(expr) {
    if expr.type == "list" {
        give { found: true, value: length(expr.items) }
    }
    if expr.type == "text" {
        give { found: true, value: length(expr.value) }
    }
    give { found: false, value: 0 }
}

// Whether an expression provably holds an empty list.
make chkIsEmptyListExpr(expr, e) {
    if expr.type == "list" {
        give length(expr.items) == 0
    }
    if expr.type == "variable" {
        give envIsProvenEmpty(e, expr.name)
    }
    give false
}

// A list value handed on under a second name: the old
// name's facts are given up.
make chkNoteAliasing(expr, e) {
    if expr.type == "variable" {
        envForgetFacts(e, expr.name)
    }
}

// A 'repeat' count the checker can prove is negative.
make chkCheckRepeatCount(count, e, line) {
    let v = chkConstNumber(count, e)
    if v.found {
        if v.value < 0 {
            give errAt(line, "'repeat' was told to run " + numText(v.value) + " times, but a count can't be negative — a loop can't run less than zero times. Use zero or more, like: repeat 3 { ... } I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
        }
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// A division whose second number is provably zero.
make chkCheckDivisor(op, right, e, line) {
    if op != "/" {
        give okVal("")
    }
    let v = chkConstNumber(right, e)
    if v.found {
        if v.value == 0 {
            give errAt(line, "I can't divide by zero. Dividing needs a second number that isn't zero. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
        }
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// An index the checker can prove lands outside.
make chkCheckIndexRange(base, baseKind, index, e, line) {
    let whole = chkWholeOf(chkConstNumber(index, e))
    let suffix = " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran."
    if base.type == "text" {
        let len = length(base.value)
        if len == 0 {
            if whole.found {
                give errAt(line, "This text has no characters yet, so there is no character " + numText(whole.value) + " to take from it." + suffix)
            }
            give errAt(line, "This text is empty — it has no characters — so no number can pick a character from it." + suffix)
        }
        if whole.found {
            if whole.value < 0 {
                give errAt(line, "This text has " + len + " characters, numbered 0 to " + (len - 1) + ", but I was asked for character " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
            }
            if whole.value >= len {
                give errAt(line, "This text has " + len + " characters, numbered 0 to " + (len - 1) + ", but I was asked for character " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if base.type == "list" {
        let count = length(base.items)
        if count == 0 {
            if whole.found {
                give errAt(line, "This list has no items yet, so there is no item " + numText(whole.value) + " to take from it. Items can be added with append — like: append(items, ...)." + suffix)
            }
            give errAt(line, "This list is empty — it has no items — so no number can pick an item from it. Items can be added with append — like: append(items, ...)." + suffix)
        }
        if whole.found {
            if whole.value < 0 {
                give errAt(line, "This list has " + count + " items, numbered 0 to " + (count - 1) + ", but I was asked for item " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
            }
            if whole.value >= count {
                give errAt(line, "This list has " + count + " items, numbered 0 to " + (count - 1) + ", but I was asked for item " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if base.type == "variable" {
        if baseKind == "list" {
            if envIsProvenEmpty(e, base.name) {
                if whole.found {
                    give errAt(line, "This list has no items yet, so there is no item " + numText(whole.value) + " to take from it. Items can be added with append — like: append(items, ...)." + suffix)
                }
                give errAt(line, "This list is empty — it has no items — so no number can pick an item from it. Items can be added with append — like: append(items, ...)." + suffix)
            }
        }
        let tracked = envLookupLength(e, base.name)
        if tracked.found {
            if baseKind == "list" {
                let len2 = tracked.value
                if len2 == 0 {
                    if whole.found {
                        give errAt(line, "This list has no items yet, so there is no item " + numText(whole.value) + " to take from it. Items can be added with append — like: append(items, ...)." + suffix)
                    }
                    give errAt(line, "This list is empty — it has no items — so no number can pick an item from it. Items can be added with append — like: append(items, ...)." + suffix)
                }
                if whole.found {
                    if whole.value < 0 {
                        give errAt(line, "This list has " + len2 + " items, numbered 0 to " + (len2 - 1) + ", but I was asked for item " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
                    }
                    if whole.value >= len2 {
                        give errAt(line, "This list has " + len2 + " items, numbered 0 to " + (len2 - 1) + ", but I was asked for item " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
                    }
                }
                give okVal("")
            }
            if baseKind == "text" {
                let len3 = tracked.value
                if len3 == 0 {
                    if whole.found {
                        give errAt(line, "This text has no characters yet, so there is no character " + numText(whole.value) + " to take from it." + suffix)
                    }
                    give errAt(line, "This text is empty — it has no characters — so no number can pick a character from it." + suffix)
                }
                if whole.found {
                    if whole.value < 0 {
                        give errAt(line, "This text has " + len3 + " characters, numbered 0 to " + (len3 - 1) + ", but I was asked for character " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
                    }
                    if whole.value >= len3 {
                        give errAt(line, "This text has " + len3 + " characters, numbered 0 to " + (len3 - 1) + ", but I was asked for character " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
                    }
                }
                give okVal("")
            }
        }
    }
    if whole.found {
        if whole.value < 0 {
            if baseKind == "list" {
                give errAt(line, "Items are numbered starting at 0, so item " + numText(whole.value) + " can never be taken — there is nothing before the first one." + suffix)
            }
            if baseKind == "text" {
                give errAt(line, "Items are numbered starting at 0, so item " + numText(whole.value) + " can never be taken — there is nothing before the first one." + suffix)
            }
            if baseKind == "bytes" {
                give errAt(line, "Items are numbered starting at 0, so item " + numText(whole.value) + " can never be taken — there is nothing before the first one." + suffix)
            }
        }
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// The ranges of the built-in tools the checker can prove.
make chkCheckBuiltinRanges(name, args, e, line) {
    let suffix = " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran."
    if name == "makebytes" {
        if length(args) == 1 {
            let v = chkConstNumber(args[0], e)
            if v.found {
                if v.value < 0 {
                    give errAt(line, "'makebytes' was told to make " + numText(v.value) + " bytes, but a count can't be less than 0." + suffix)
                }
                if v.value > 16777216 {
                    give errAt(line, "'makebytes' was told to make " + numText(v.value) + " bytes, but one block can hold at most 16777216 bytes." + suffix)
                }
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if name == "exitwith" {
        if length(args) == 1 {
            let v2 = chkConstNumber(args[0], e)
            if v2.found {
                if v2.value != rounddown(v2.value) {
                    give errAt(line, "'exitwith' needs a whole number from 0 to 255, but it was given " + numText(v2.value) + "." + suffix)
                }
                if v2.value < 0 {
                    give errAt(line, "'exitwith' needs a whole number from 0 to 255, but it was given " + numText(v2.value) + "." + suffix)
                }
                if v2.value > 255 {
                    give errAt(line, "'exitwith' needs a whole number from 0 to 255, but it was given " + numText(v2.value) + "." + suffix)
                }
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if name == "removeat" {
        if length(args) == 2 {
            let whole = chkWholeOf(chkConstNumber(args[1], e))
            let lenObj = { found: false, value: 0 }
            let provenEmpty = false
            if args[0].type == "list" {
                lenObj = { found: true, value: length(args[0].items) }
                provenEmpty = length(args[0].items) == 0
            }
            if args[0].type == "variable" {
                provenEmpty = envIsProvenEmpty(e, args[0].name)
                lenObj = envLookupLength(e, args[0].name)
            }
            if provenEmpty {
                if whole.found {
                    give errAt(line, "This list has no items yet, so there is no item " + numText(whole.value) + " to remove." + suffix)
                }
                give errAt(line, "This list is empty — it has no items — so there is no item to remove from it." + suffix)
            }
            if lenObj.found {
                if whole.found {
                    if whole.value < 0 {
                        give errAt(line, "This list has " + lenObj.value + " items, numbered 0 to " + (lenObj.value - 1) + ", but I was asked to remove item " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
                    }
                    if whole.value >= lenObj.value {
                        give errAt(line, "This list has " + lenObj.value + " items, numbered 0 to " + (lenObj.value - 1) + ", but I was asked to remove item " + numText(whole.value) + "." + suffix)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if name == "part" {
        if length(args) == 3 {
            if args[0].type == "text" {
                let len = length(args[0].value)
                let start = chkWholeOf(chkConstNumber(args[1], e))
                let count = chkWholeOf(chkConstNumber(args[2], e))
                if start.found {
                    if start.value < 0 {
                        give errAt(line, "'part' was told to start at character " + numText(start.value) + ", but the first character is number 0 — a start can't be less than 0." + suffix)
                    }
                }
                if count.found {
                    if count.value < 0 {
                        give errAt(line, "'part' was told to take " + numText(count.value) + " characters, but a count can't be less than 0." + suffix)
                    }
                }
                if start.found {
                    if start.value > len {
                        if len == 0 {
                            give errAt(line, "'part' was told to start at character " + numText(start.value) + ", but the text has no characters yet." + suffix)
                        }
                        give errAt(line, "'part' was told to start at character " + numText(start.value) + ", but the text has only " + len + " characters, numbered 0 to " + (len - 1) + "." + suffix)
                    }
                }
                if start.found {
                    if count.found {
                        if len > 0 {
                            if start.value + count.value > len {
                                give errAt(line, "'part' was told to take " + numText(count.value) + " characters starting at character " + numText(start.value) + ", but the text has only " + len + " characters, numbered 0 to " + (len - 1) + " — that runs past the end." + suffix)
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// When a condition tests a 'maybe' variable against
// 'nothing', the kind each road may narrow it to —
// nothingTest. { found, name, thenKind, elseKind }.
make chkNothingTest(condition, e) {
    let none = { found: false, name: "", thenKind: "", elseKind: "" }
    if condition.type != "binary" {
        give none
    }
    if condition.op != "==" {
        if condition.op != "!=" {
            give none
        }
    }
    let sides = [[condition.left, condition.right], [condition.right, condition.left]]
    let si = 0
    while si < 2 {
        let varSide = sides[si][0]
        let otherSide = sides[si][1]
        if varSide.type == "variable" {
            let looked = envLookup(e, varSide.name)
            if looked.found {
                let kind = looked.value
                let inner = ""
                let hasInner = false
                if chkIsMaybe(kind) {
                    inner = chkMaybeInner(kind)
                    hasInner = true
                } else {
                    let ann = envLookupAnn(e, varSide.name)
                    if ann.found {
                        let marked = chkFromWord(ann.value)
                        if marked != "" {
                            if chkIsMaybe(marked) {
                                if kind == "nothing" {
                                    inner = chkMaybeInner(marked)
                                    hasInner = true
                                }
                                if kind == chkMaybeInner(marked) {
                                    inner = chkMaybeInner(marked)
                                    hasInner = true
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                if hasInner {
                    let otherIsNothing = false
                    if otherSide.type == "nothing" {
                        otherIsNothing = true
                    }
                    if otherSide.type == "variable" {
                        let otherLooked = envLookup(e, otherSide.name)
                        if otherLooked.found {
                            if otherLooked.value == "nothing" {
                                otherIsNothing = true
                            }
                        }
                    }
                    if otherIsNothing {
                        if condition.op == "==" {
                            give { found: true, name: varSide.name, thenKind: "nothing", elseKind: inner }
                        }
                        give { found: true, name: varSide.name, thenKind: inner, elseKind: "nothing" }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        si = si + 1
    }
    give none
}

// An index whose base or pick is proven nothing, or may
// be — checkIndexKind.
make chkCheckIndexKind(base, index, line) {
    if base == "nothing" {
        give errAt(line, "I can't take an item from nothing. Only a list or some text has items to take from — like [1, 2, 3], where [0] takes the first item, or \"abc\", where [0] takes the first character. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(base) {
        give chkUnhandledMaybe(base, line)
    }
    if index == "nothing" {
        give errAt(line, "I can't use nothing to pick an item. Items are picked by a whole number — 0 for the first one, 1 for the second, and so on. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(index) {
        give chkUnhandledMaybe(index, line)
    }
    if base == "list" {
        if index != "unknown" {
            if index != "number" {
                give errAt(line, "I can't use " + chkPhrase(index) + " to pick an item. Items are picked by a whole number — 0 for the first one, 1 for the second, and so on. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if base == "text" {
        if index != "unknown" {
            if index != "number" {
                give errAt(line, "I can't use " + chkPhrase(index) + " to pick an item. Items are picked by a whole number — 0 for the first one, 1 for the second, and so on. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if base == "bytes" {
        if index != "unknown" {
            if index != "number" {
                give errAt(line, "I can't use " + chkPhrase(index) + " to pick an item. Items are picked by a whole number — 0 for the first one, 1 for the second, and so on. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// Reading a field of the empty value, or of a maybe —
// checkFieldBase.
make chkCheckFieldBase(base, fieldName, line) {
    if base == "nothing" {
        give errAt(line, "I can't read the field '" + fieldName + "' of nothing, because only an object has fields. Make an object first, like: let person = { name: \"Tyler\" } — then person." + fieldName + " works. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(base) {
        give chkUnhandledMaybe(base, line)
    }
    give okVal("")
}

make chkCheckCountKind(kind, line) {
    if kind == "nothing" {
        give errAt(line, "'repeat' needs a number saying how many times to run, but here it was given nothing. Write it like: repeat 3 { ... } I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(kind) {
        give chkUnhandledMaybe(kind, line)
    }
    give okVal("")
}

make chkCheckWalkKind(kind, line) {
    if kind == "nothing" {
        give errAt(line, "'for' needs a list, some text, or a block of bytes to walk through, but here it was given nothing. Write it like: for item in items { ... } I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(kind) {
        give chkUnhandledMaybe(kind, line)
    }
    give okVal("")
}

make chkCheckNegate(kind, line) {
    if kind == "nothing" {
        give errAt(line, "I can only put a minus sign in front of a number, but here it was in front of nothing. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
    }
    if chkIsMaybe(kind) {
        give chkUnhandledMaybe(kind, line)
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// The verb an arithmetic operator is refused with.
make chkVerbOf(op) {
    let verb = "divide"
    if op == "-" {
        verb = "subtract"
    }
    if op == "*" {
        verb = "multiply"
    }
    give verb
}

// The refusal for an operation with the empty value on a
// side — checkBinary's nothing road, message only (""
// when the operator has no such refusal).
make chkNothingBinaryMessage(op, left, right) {
    let note = " Nothing is the empty value: there is nothing there to work with. If a value might be nothing, check it first — if value == nothing { ... } else { ... } — and use it in the 'else', where it is known to be there."
    let msg = ""
    if op == "+" {
        msg = "I can't add " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '+' adds two numbers, or joins text together." + note + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran."
    }
    if op == "-" {
        msg = "I can't subtract " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '-' works on two numbers. (Only '+' joins text.)" + note + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran."
    }
    if op == "*" {
        msg = "I can't multiply " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '*' works on two numbers. (Only '+' joins text.)" + note + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran."
    }
    if op == "/" {
        msg = "I can't divide " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '/' works on two numbers. (Only '+' joins text.)" + note + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran."
    }
    let isCmp = false
    if op == "<" {
        isCmp = true
    }
    if op == "<=" {
        isCmp = true
    }
    if op == ">" {
        isCmp = true
    }
    if op == ">=" {
        isCmp = true
    }
    if isCmp {
        msg = "I can't compare " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + " with '" + op + "'. '" + op + "' compares two numbers. (Text and true/false values can be checked with == and !=.)" + note + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran."
    }
    give msg
}

make chkPlusResult(left, right, line) {
    let answer = okVal("unknown")
    if left != "unknown" {
        if right != "unknown" {
            if left == "text" {
                answer = okVal("text")
            } else {
                if right == "text" {
                    answer = okVal("text")
                } else {
                    if left == "number" {
                        if right == "number" {
                            answer = okVal("number")
                        } else {
                            answer = errAt(line, "I can't add " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '+' adds two numbers, or joins text together. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
                        }
                    } else {
                        answer = errAt(line, "I can't add " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '+' adds two numbers, or joins text together. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
                    }
                }
            }
        } else {
            if left == "text" {
                answer = okVal("text")
            }
            if right == "text" {
                answer = okVal("text")
            }
        }
    } else {
        if left == "text" {
            answer = okVal("text")
        }
        if right == "text" {
            answer = okVal("text")
        }
    }
    give answer
}

make chkArithResult(op, left, right, line) {
    let answer = okVal("number")
    if left != "unknown" {
        if right != "unknown" {
            if left != "number" {
                answer = errAt(line, "I can't " + chkVerbOf(op) + " " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '" + op + "' works on two numbers. (Only '+' joins text.) I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
            if right != "number" {
                answer = errAt(line, "I can't " + chkVerbOf(op) + " " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + ". '" + op + "' works on two numbers. (Only '+' joins text.) I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
        }
    }
    give answer
}

make chkCompareResult(op, left, right, line) {
    let answer = okVal("boolean")
    if left != "unknown" {
        if right != "unknown" {
            if left != "number" {
                answer = errAt(line, "I can't compare " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + " with '" + op + "'. '" + op + "' compares two numbers. (Text and true/false values can be checked with == and !=.) I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
            if right != "number" {
                answer = errAt(line, "I can't compare " + chkPhrase(left) + " and " + chkPhrase(right) + " with '" + op + "'. '" + op + "' compares two numbers. (Text and true/false values can be checked with == and !=.) I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
        }
    }
    give answer
}

// The kind rules of the operators — checkBinary. Gives
// back okVal(kind) or the refusal.
make chkCheckBinary(op, left, right, line) {
    if op != "==" {
        if op != "!=" {
            if chkIsMaybe(left) {
                let r = chkUnhandledMaybe(left, line)
                if op == "+" {
                    if left == "text" {
                        r = okVal("text")
                    }
                    if right == "text" {
                        r = okVal("text")
                    }
                }
                give r
            }
            if chkIsMaybe(right) {
                let r2 = chkUnhandledMaybe(right, line)
                if op == "+" {
                    if left == "text" {
                        r2 = okVal("text")
                    }
                    if right == "text" {
                        r2 = okVal("text")
                    }
                }
                give r2
            }
            if left == "nothing" {
                let msg = chkNothingBinaryMessage(op, left, right)
                if msg != "" {
                    let early = errAt(line, msg)
                    if op == "+" {
                        if left == "text" {
                            early = okVal("text")
                        }
                        if right == "text" {
                            early = okVal("text")
                        }
                    }
                    give early
                }
            }
            if right == "nothing" {
                let msg2 = chkNothingBinaryMessage(op, left, right)
                if msg2 != "" {
                    let early2 = errAt(line, msg2)
                    if op == "+" {
                        if left == "text" {
                            early2 = okVal("text")
                        }
                        if right == "text" {
                            early2 = okVal("text")
                        }
                    }
                    give early2
                }
            }
        }
    }
    let answer = okVal("unknown")
    if op == "+" {
        answer = chkPlusResult(left, right, line)
    }
    if op == "-" {
        answer = chkArithResult(op, left, right, line)
    }
    if op == "*" {
        answer = chkArithResult(op, left, right, line)
    }
    if op == "/" {
        answer = chkArithResult(op, left, right, line)
    }
    if op == "<" {
        answer = chkCompareResult(op, left, right, line)
    }
    if op == "<=" {
        answer = chkCompareResult(op, left, right, line)
    }
    if op == ">" {
        answer = chkCompareResult(op, left, right, line)
    }
    if op == ">=" {
        answer = chkCompareResult(op, left, right, line)
    }
    if op == "==" {
        answer = okVal("boolean")
    }
    if op == "!=" {
        answer = okVal("boolean")
    }
    give answer
}

// Whether a floor block's text is only whitespace —
// Swift's trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
// emptiness test, over the characters a floor block's
// lines can hold.
make chkAsmIsEmpty(asm) {
    let allSpace = true
    for c in asm {
        let ok = false
        if isSpaceCh(c) {
            ok = true
        }
        if c == "\n" {
            ok = true
        }
        if find(FLOORTRIM, c) >= 0 {
            ok = true
        }
        if ok == false {
            allSpace = false
        }
    }
    give allSpace
}

// ---------- calls ----------

// The bare-only tools. Stone 50: this answers "not a bare
// tool" ({ found: false }) for every name, in every mode —
// the hosted walk knows none of them, and check-bare /
// check-guest run the hosted walk in this stone. Stone 51
// extends THIS function with the bare/guest doors (the
// bareOnlyTools / guestDeskTools / guestDoorTools tables
// and their crossing refusals), in this one place.
make chkBareTool(name, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving) {
    give { found: false, kind: "unknown" }
}

// A call to a built-in tool — checkBuiltinCall. Gives
// back okVal(the kind the tool gives back) or a refusal.
make chkBuiltinCall(name, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving) {
    let def = chkBuiltinDef(name)
    let rule = chkBuiltinRule(name)
    let kinds = []
    for arg in args {
        let kr = chkExprAny(arg, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if kr.ok == false {
            give kr
        }
        append(kinds, kr.value)
    }
    if length(args) != length(def.params) {
        give errAt(line, chkBuiltinCountMessage(name, def, length(args)))
    }
    let i = 0
    while i < length(kinds) {
        let kind = kinds[i]
        if kind != "unknown" {
            let need = rule.needs[i]
            if need.hasAllowed {
                if nameIndex(need.allowed, kind) < 0 {
                    let message = "'" + name + "' needs " + need.need + ", but it was given " + chkPhrase(kind) + "."
                    if chkIsMaybe(kind) {
                        message = message + " A value that may be nothing has to be checked first: if value == nothing { ... } else { ... } — in the 'else', the value is known to be there."
                    }
                    give errAt(line, message + " I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let rr = chkCheckBuiltinRanges(name, args, e, line)
    if rr.ok == false {
        give rr
    }
    if name == "wholediv" {
        let dr = chkCheckDivisor("/", args[1], e, line)
        if dr.ok == false {
            give dr
        }
    }
    if name == "leftover" {
        let dr2 = chkCheckDivisor("/", args[1], e, line)
        if dr2.ok == false {
            give dr2
        }
    }
    if name == "append" {
        if args[0].type == "variable" {
            envForgetFacts(e, args[0].name)
        }
    }
    if name == "insertat" {
        if args[0].type == "variable" {
            envForgetFacts(e, args[0].name)
        }
    }
    if name == "removeat" {
        if args[0].type == "variable" {
            envForgetFacts(e, args[0].name)
        }
    }
    give okVal(rule.result)
}

// A call to a function the program made — the part of
// checkCall past the built-in and bare doors.
make chkCallMade(name, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving) {
    let rec = nmGet(gChkFuncs, name)
    if rec.found == false {
        let message = "I don't know a function called '" + name + "' yet. You can make one with: make " + name + "(...) { ... }"
        let suggestion = chkClosestFunction(name)
        if suggestion.found {
            message = message + " Did you mean '" + suggestion.name + "'?"
        }
        give errAt(line, message)
    }
    let record = rec.value
    let argKinds = []
    for arg in args {
        let kr = chkExprAny(arg, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if kr.ok == false {
            give kr
        }
        append(argKinds, kr.value)
    }
    if length(args) != length(record.params) {
        give errAt(line, "The function '" + name + "' takes " + chkTakesText(record.params) + ", but it was given " + length(args) + ". Make the call match how the function was made: " + name + "(" + join(record.params, ", ") + ").")
    }
    let ar = chkCheckCallAnnotations(name, record, argKinds, line)
    if ar.ok == false {
        give ar
    }
    envForgetKinds(e)
    give okVal("")
}

// A call, by any road — checkCall / checkCallForgiving
// share this shape, the forgiving flag choosing the
// expression walk, as Swift's two functions differ only
// in that walk.
make chkCallAny(name, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving) {
    let def = chkBuiltinDef(name)
    if def.found {
        let br = chkBuiltinCall(name, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if br.ok == false {
            give br
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    let bare = chkBareTool(name, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving)
    if bare.found {
        give okVal("")
    }
    give chkCallMade(name, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving)
}

// A floor block — checkFloor, the hosted side: the block
// must not be empty, each handed value a number or a
// block of bytes, at most one block of bytes; what it
// gives back can't be proven, so the kind is unknown.
// (The bare branches — a piece of text allowed, a number
// given back — land in Stone 51 with the doors.)
make chkFloor(asm, args, line, e, outerNames, forgiving) {
    if chkAsmIsEmpty(asm) {
        give errAt(line, "This floor block is empty — it needs the processor instructions it is meant to run, between the 'floor {' line and the line holding only '}'.")
    }
    let bytesSeen = 0
    for arg in args {
        let kr = chkExprAny(arg, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if kr.ok == false {
            give kr
        }
        let kind = kr.value
        if kind == "bytes" {
            bytesSeen = bytesSeen + 1
            if bytesSeen > 1 {
                give errAt(line, "'floor' can be handed only one block of bytes, but it was given two. I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
            }
        } else {
            if kind != "unknown" {
                if kind != "number" {
                    give errAt(line, "'floor' can be handed numbers and one block of bytes, but it was given " + chkPhrase(kind) + ". I caught this before the program ran, so nothing ran.")
                }
            }
        }
    }
    give okVal("unknown")
}

// ---------- expressions ----------
// checkExpr and checkExprForgiving share one body here,
// the forgiving flag choosing — at exactly the places
// Swift's two functions differ — how a variable found
// nowhere is read (forgiven when a 'let' for it exists
// around the function's making) and which walk the parts
// below take. Swift duplicates the walk; the parts where
// the two walks are word-for-word the same are one road
// here, and the parts where they differ (the variable
// case, and the recursion itself) branch on the flag.

make chkExprAny(expr, e, outerNames, forgiving) {
    let litKind = ""
    if expr.type == "number" {
        litKind = "number"
    }
    if expr.type == "text" {
        litKind = "text"
    }
    if expr.type == "boolean" {
        litKind = "boolean"
    }
    if expr.type == "nothing" {
        litKind = "nothing"
    }
    if litKind != "" {
        give okVal(litKind)
    }
    if expr.type == "variable" {
        let looked = envLookup(e, expr.name)
        if looked.found {
            give okVal(looked.value)
        }
        if forgiving {
            if chkContainsName(outerNames, expr.name) {
                give okVal("unknown")
            }
        }
        let message = "I don't know a variable called '" + expr.name + "' yet. You can make one with: let " + expr.name + " = ..."
        let suggestion = chkClosestName(expr.name, e)
        if suggestion.found {
            message = message + " Did you mean '" + suggestion.name + "'?"
        }
        give errAt(expr.line, message)
    }
    if expr.type == "call" {
        let def = chkBuiltinDef(expr.name)
        if def.found {
            give chkBuiltinCall(expr.name, expr.args, expr.line, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        }
        let bare = chkBareTool(expr.name, expr.args, expr.line, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if bare.found {
            give okVal(bare.kind)
        }
        let cr = chkCallMade(expr.name, expr.args, expr.line, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if cr.ok == false {
            give cr
        }
        let rec = nmGet(gChkFuncs, expr.name)
        if rec.found {
            let rk = rec.value.returnKind
            if rk != "" {
                let kind = chkFromWord(rk)
                if kind != "" {
                    give okVal(kind)
                }
            }
        }
        give okVal("unknown")
    }
    if expr.type == "object" {
        for v in expr.values {
            let vr = chkExprAny(v, e, outerNames, forgiving)
            if vr.ok == false {
                give vr
            }
        }
        give okVal("object")
    }
    if expr.type == "list" {
        for item in expr.items {
            let ir = chkExprAny(item, e, outerNames, forgiving)
            if ir.ok == false {
                give ir
            }
        }
        give okVal("list")
    }
    if expr.type == "field" {
        let br = chkExprAny(expr.base, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if br.ok == false {
            give br
        }
        let fr = chkCheckFieldBase(br.value, expr.name, expr.line)
        if fr.ok == false {
            give fr
        }
        give okVal("unknown")
    }
    if expr.type == "index" {
        let br2 = chkExprAny(expr.base, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if br2.ok == false {
            give br2
        }
        let ir2 = chkExprAny(expr.index, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if ir2.ok == false {
            give ir2
        }
        let kr = chkCheckIndexKind(br2.value, ir2.value, expr.line)
        if kr.ok == false {
            give kr
        }
        let rr = chkCheckIndexRange(expr.base, br2.value, expr.index, e, expr.line)
        if rr.ok == false {
            give rr
        }
        if br2.value == "text" {
            give okVal("text")
        }
        give okVal("unknown")
    }
    if expr.type == "floor" {
        give chkFloor(expr.asm, expr.args, expr.line, e, outerNames, forgiving)
    }
    if expr.type == "negate" {
        let nr = chkExprAny(expr.inner, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if nr.ok == false {
            give nr
        }
        let cr2 = chkCheckNegate(nr.value, expr.line)
        if cr2.ok == false {
            give cr2
        }
        if nr.value == "number" {
            give okVal("number")
        }
        give okVal("unknown")
    }
    if expr.type == "binary" {
        let lr = chkExprAny(expr.left, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if lr.ok == false {
            give lr
        }
        let rr2 = chkExprAny(expr.right, e, outerNames, forgiving)
        if rr2.ok == false {
            give rr2
        }
        let dr = chkCheckDivisor(expr.op, expr.right, e, expr.line)
        if dr.ok == false {
            give dr
        }
        give chkCheckBinary(expr.op, lr.value, rr2.value, expr.line)
    }
    give okVal("unknown")
}

// ---------- statements: the plain walk ----------
// checkBlock / checkChildBlock / checkStatement, and
// checkDefine. The forgiving twins follow in their own
// section, duplicated as Swift duplicates them — the two
// walks differ (the attempt/for/while child handling in
// the forgiving walk, the unmade-name forgiveness), and
// the duplication is mirrored rather than unified.

make chkAnnotationWording(name, kindWord, kind) {
    give "'" + name + "' is marked as " + chkPhraseForWord(kindWord) + " by its annotation, but this value is " + chkPhrase(kind) + ". Change the value, or change the annotation."
}

make chkBlock(stmts, e, outerNames) {
    let outer = chkUnionNames(outerNames, chkDeclaredNames(stmts))
    for s in stmts {
        let r = chkStatement(s, e, outer)
        if r.ok == false {
            give r
        }
    }
    give okVal("")
}

make chkChildBlock(stmts, e, outerNames, mergeWithBefore) {
    let before = envCopy(e)
    envPush(e)
    let r = chkBlock(stmts, e, outerNames)
    if r.ok == false {
        give r
    }
    envPop(e)
    if mergeWithBefore {
        envBecome(e, envMerged(before, e))
    }
    give okVal("")
}

make chkStatement(s, e, outerNames) {
    if s.type == "declare" {
        let kr = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, false)
        if kr.ok == false {
            give kr
        }
        if s.kindWord != "" {
            let ar = chkCheckAnnotation(s.kindWord, kr.value, s.line, chkAnnotationWording(s.name, s.kindWord, kr.value))
            if ar.ok == false {
                give ar
            }
        }
        let constObj = chkConstNumber(s.value, e)
        let provenEmpty = chkIsEmptyListExpr(s.value, e)
        chkNoteAliasing(s.value, e)
        envDeclare(e, s.name, chkDeclaredKind(s.kindWord, kr.value), s.kindWord, constObj, provenEmpty, chkLiteralLength(s.value))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "assign" {
        let kr2 = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, false)
        if kr2.ok == false {
            give kr2
        }
        let looked = envLookup(e, s.name)
        if looked.found == false {
            give errAt(s.line, "I can't set '" + s.name + "', because no variable called '" + s.name + "' has been made yet. Make one first with: let " + s.name + " = ...")
        }
        let ann = envLookupAnn(e, s.name)
        let annWord = ""
        if ann.found {
            annWord = ann.value
            let ar2 = chkCheckAnnotation(annWord, kr2.value, s.line, chkAnnotationWording(s.name, annWord, kr2.value))
            if ar2.ok == false {
                give ar2
            }
        }
        let constObj2 = chkConstNumber(s.value, e)
        let provenEmpty2 = chkIsEmptyListExpr(s.value, e)
        chkNoteAliasing(s.value, e)
        envAssign(e, s.name, chkDeclaredKind(annWord, kr2.value), constObj2, provenEmpty2, chkLiteralLength(s.value))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "assignTarget" {
        let tr = chkExprAny(s.target, e, outerNames, false)
        if tr.ok == false {
            give tr
        }
        let vr = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, false)
        if vr.ok == false {
            give vr
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "say" {
        let vr2 = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, false)
        if vr2.ok == false {
            give vr2
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "if" {
        let cr = chkExprAny(s.condition, e, outerNames, false)
        if cr.ok == false {
            give cr
        }
        let test = chkNothingTest(s.condition, e)
        let before = envCopy(e)
        if test.found {
            envNarrow(e, test.name, test.thenKind)
        }
        let tr2 = chkChildBlock(s.thenStmts, e, outerNames, false)
        if tr2.ok == false {
            give tr2
        }
        let afterThen = envCopy(e)
        envBecome(e, before)
        if test.found {
            envNarrow(e, test.name, test.elseKind)
        }
        if s.hasElse {
            let er = chkChildBlock(s.elseStmts, e, outerNames, false)
            if er.ok == false {
                give er
            }
        }
        envBecome(e, envMerged(afterThen, e))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "while" {
        let cr3 = chkExprAny(s.condition, e, outerNames, false)
        if cr3.ok == false {
            give cr3
        }
        let test2 = chkNothingTest(s.condition, e)
        if test2.found {
            envNarrow(e, test2.name, test2.thenKind)
        }
        let br = chkChildBlock(s.body, e, outerNames, true)
        if br.ok == false {
            give br
        }
        if test2.found {
            envNarrow(e, test2.name, test2.elseKind)
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "repeat" {
        let kr3 = chkExprAny(s.count, e, outerNames, false)
        if kr3.ok == false {
            give kr3
        }
        let rr = chkCheckRepeatCount(s.count, e, s.line)
        if rr.ok == false {
            give rr
        }
        let ck = chkCheckCountKind(kr3.value, s.line)
        if ck.ok == false {
            give ck
        }
        let br2 = chkChildBlock(s.body, e, outerNames, true)
        if br2.ok == false {
            give br2
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "for" {
        let kr4 = chkExprAny(s.over, e, outerNames, false)
        if kr4.ok == false {
            give kr4
        }
        let wk = chkCheckWalkKind(kr4.value, s.line)
        if wk.ok == false {
            give wk
        }
        let before2 = envCopy(e)
        envPush(e)
        envDeclare(e, s.name, "unknown", "", { found: false, value: 0 }, false, { found: false, value: 0 })
        let br3 = chkBlock(s.body, e, outerNames)
        if br3.ok == false {
            give br3
        }
        envPop(e)
        envBecome(e, envMerged(before2, e))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "define" {
        give chkDefine(s.name, s.params, s.paramKinds, s.returnKind, s.body, e, outerNames)
    }
    if s.type == "give" {
        let kr5 = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, false)
        if kr5.ok == false {
            give kr5
        }
        give chkCheckGive(kr5.value, s.line)
    }
    if s.type == "attempt" {
        let before3 = envCopy(e)
        let br4 = chkChildBlock(s.body, e, outerNames, false)
        if br4.ok == false {
            give br4
        }
        let afterBody = envCopy(e)
        envBecome(e, before3)
        envPush(e)
        envDeclare(e, "problem", "text", "", { found: false, value: 0 }, false, { found: false, value: 0 })
        let rr2 = chkBlock(s.recovery, e, outerNames)
        if rr2.ok == false {
            give rr2
        }
        envPop(e)
        envBecome(e, envMerged(afterBody, e))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "doCall" {
        give chkCallAny(s.name, s.args, s.line, e, outerNames, false)
    }
    if s.type == "doExpr" {
        let er2 = chkExprAny(s.expr, e, outerNames, false)
        if er2.ok == false {
            give er2
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// A function body is checked where the function is made —
// checkDefine. The body's env starts as a copy of the
// making env with its facts dropped; what the body may
// have changed is not carried back.
make chkDefine(name, params, paramKinds, returnKind, body, e, outerNames) {
    let bodyEnv = envCopy(e)
    envDropFacts(bodyEnv)
    envPush(bodyEnv)
    let i = 0
    while i < length(params) {
        let kindWord = ""
        if i < length(paramKinds) {
            kindWord = paramKinds[i]
        }
        envDeclare(bodyEnv, params[i], chkDeclaredKind(kindWord, "unknown"), kindWord, { found: false, value: 0 }, false, { found: false, value: 0 })
        i = i + 1
    }
    let bodyOuter = chkUnionNames(outerNames, chkDeclaredNames(body))
    let savedReturnKind = gChkReturnKind
    let savedFuncName = gChkFuncName
    gChkReturnKind = returnKind
    gChkFuncName = name
    let result = okVal("")
    for s in body {
        let r = chkStatementInFunction(s, bodyEnv, bodyOuter)
        if r.ok == false {
            result = r
        }
        if result.ok == false {
            gChkReturnKind = savedReturnKind
            gChkFuncName = savedFuncName
            give result
        }
    }
    gChkReturnKind = savedReturnKind
    gChkFuncName = savedFuncName
    give okVal("")
}

// ---------- statements: the forgiving walk (function bodies) ----------
// checkStatementInFunction / checkChildBlockInFunction /
// checkStatementWithForgiveness, duplicated from the
// plain walk as Swift duplicates them. The differences
// are Swift's own: an unmade name found in outerNames is
// forgiven; the attempt body and recovery are walked
// statement by statement in pushed scopes (not through
// the child-block helper); the for body likewise, with
// its own outer-names union; the while and repeat bodies
// go through the child-block helper and merge with the
// state from BEFORE the narrowing (while) / before the
// body (repeat).

make chkStatementInFunction(s, e, outerNames) {
    if s.type == "define" {
        give chkDefine(s.name, s.params, s.paramKinds, s.returnKind, s.body, e, outerNames)
    }
    give chkStatementForgiving(s, e, outerNames)
}

make chkChildBlockInFunction(stmts, e, outerNames) {
    envPush(e)
    let outer = chkUnionNames(outerNames, chkDeclaredNames(stmts))
    let result = okVal("")
    for s in stmts {
        let r = chkStatementInFunction(s, e, outer)
        if r.ok == false {
            result = r
        }
        if result.ok == false {
            envPop(e)
            give result
        }
    }
    envPop(e)
    give okVal("")
}

make chkStatementForgiving(s, e, outerNames) {
    if s.type == "declare" {
        let kr = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, true)
        if kr.ok == false {
            give kr
        }
        if s.kindWord != "" {
            let ar = chkCheckAnnotation(s.kindWord, kr.value, s.line, chkAnnotationWording(s.name, s.kindWord, kr.value))
            if ar.ok == false {
                give ar
            }
        }
        let constObj = chkConstNumber(s.value, e)
        let provenEmpty = chkIsEmptyListExpr(s.value, e)
        chkNoteAliasing(s.value, e)
        envDeclare(e, s.name, chkDeclaredKind(s.kindWord, kr.value), s.kindWord, constObj, provenEmpty, chkLiteralLength(s.value))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "assign" {
        let kr2 = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, true)
        if kr2.ok == false {
            give kr2
        }
        let looked = envLookup(e, s.name)
        if looked.found == false {
            if chkContainsName(outerNames, s.name) == false {
                give errAt(s.line, "I can't set '" + s.name + "', because no variable called '" + s.name + "' has been made yet. Make one first with: let " + s.name + " = ...")
            }
        }
        let ann = envLookupAnn(e, s.name)
        let annWord = ""
        if ann.found {
            annWord = ann.value
            let ar2 = chkCheckAnnotation(annWord, kr2.value, s.line, chkAnnotationWording(s.name, annWord, kr2.value))
            if ar2.ok == false {
                give ar2
            }
        }
        let constObj2 = chkConstNumber(s.value, e)
        let provenEmpty2 = chkIsEmptyListExpr(s.value, e)
        chkNoteAliasing(s.value, e)
        envAssign(e, s.name, chkDeclaredKind(annWord, kr2.value), constObj2, provenEmpty2, chkLiteralLength(s.value))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "assignTarget" {
        let tr = chkExprAny(s.target, e, outerNames, true)
        if tr.ok == false {
            give tr
        }
        let vr = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, true)
        if vr.ok == false {
            give vr
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "say" {
        let vr2 = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, true)
        if vr2.ok == false {
            give vr2
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "if" {
        let cr = chkExprAny(s.condition, e, outerNames, true)
        if cr.ok == false {
            give cr
        }
        let test = chkNothingTest(s.condition, e)
        let before = envCopy(e)
        if test.found {
            envNarrow(e, test.name, test.thenKind)
        }
        let tr2 = chkChildBlockInFunction(s.thenStmts, e, outerNames)
        if tr2.ok == false {
            give tr2
        }
        let afterThen = envCopy(e)
        envBecome(e, before)
        if test.found {
            envNarrow(e, test.name, test.elseKind)
        }
        if s.hasElse {
            let er = chkChildBlockInFunction(s.elseStmts, e, outerNames)
            if er.ok == false {
                give er
            }
        }
        envBecome(e, envMerged(afterThen, e))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "while" {
        let cr3 = chkExprAny(s.condition, e, outerNames, true)
        if cr3.ok == false {
            give cr3
        }
        let test2 = chkNothingTest(s.condition, e)
        let before2 = envCopy(e)
        if test2.found {
            envNarrow(e, test2.name, test2.thenKind)
        }
        let br = chkChildBlockInFunction(s.body, e, outerNames)
        if br.ok == false {
            give br
        }
        envBecome(e, envMerged(before2, e))
        if test2.found {
            envNarrow(e, test2.name, test2.elseKind)
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "repeat" {
        let kr3 = chkExprAny(s.count, e, outerNames, true)
        if kr3.ok == false {
            give kr3
        }
        let rr = chkCheckRepeatCount(s.count, e, s.line)
        if rr.ok == false {
            give rr
        }
        let ck = chkCheckCountKind(kr3.value, s.line)
        if ck.ok == false {
            give ck
        }
        let before3 = envCopy(e)
        let br2 = chkChildBlockInFunction(s.body, e, outerNames)
        if br2.ok == false {
            give br2
        }
        envBecome(e, envMerged(before3, e))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "for" {
        let kr4 = chkExprAny(s.over, e, outerNames, true)
        if kr4.ok == false {
            give kr4
        }
        let wk = chkCheckWalkKind(kr4.value, s.line)
        if wk.ok == false {
            give wk
        }
        let before4 = envCopy(e)
        envPush(e)
        envDeclare(e, s.name, "unknown", "", { found: false, value: 0 }, false, { found: false, value: 0 })
        let outer2 = chkUnionNames(outerNames, chkDeclaredNames(s.body))
        let result2 = okVal("")
        for inner in s.body {
            let ir = chkStatementInFunction(inner, e, outer2)
            if ir.ok == false {
                result2 = ir
            }
            if result2.ok == false {
                envPop(e)
                give result2
            }
        }
        envPop(e)
        envBecome(e, envMerged(before4, e))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "define" {
        give chkDefine(s.name, s.params, s.paramKinds, s.returnKind, s.body, e, outerNames)
    }
    if s.type == "give" {
        let kr5 = chkExprAny(s.value, e, outerNames, true)
        if kr5.ok == false {
            give kr5
        }
        give chkCheckGive(kr5.value, s.line)
    }
    if s.type == "attempt" {
        let before5 = envCopy(e)
        envPush(e)
        let bodyOuter = chkUnionNames(outerNames, chkDeclaredNames(s.body))
        let result3 = okVal("")
        for inner2 in s.body {
            let ir2 = chkStatementInFunction(inner2, e, bodyOuter)
            if ir2.ok == false {
                result3 = ir2
            }
            if result3.ok == false {
                envPop(e)
                give result3
            }
        }
        envPop(e)
        let afterBody = envCopy(e)
        envBecome(e, before5)
        envPush(e)
        envDeclare(e, "problem", "text", "", { found: false, value: 0 }, false, { found: false, value: 0 })
        let recoveryOuter = chkUnionNames(outerNames, chkDeclaredNames(s.recovery))
        for inner3 in s.recovery {
            let ir3 = chkStatementInFunction(inner3, e, recoveryOuter)
            if ir3.ok == false {
                envPop(e)
                give ir3
            }
        }
        envPop(e)
        envBecome(e, envMerged(afterBody, e))
        give okVal("")
    }
    if s.type == "doCall" {
        give chkCallAny(s.name, s.args, s.line, e, outerNames, true)
    }
    if s.type == "doExpr" {
        let er2 = chkExprAny(s.expr, e, outerNames, true)
        if er2.ok == false {
            give er2
        }
        give okVal("")
    }
    give okVal("")
}

// The checker's door: collect the functions, then walk
// the program in a fresh environment.
make chkCheckProgram(stmts) {
    nmClear(gChkFuncs)
    chkCollectFunctions(stmts)
    gChkReturnKind = ""
    gChkFuncName = ""
    let e = envNew()
    give chkBlock(stmts, e, [])
}

// ---------- the program itself ----------
// What running this front end does: take the mode and the
// file path from the words it was started with, read the
// file, and print what the mode asks for — the tokens
// ('tokens', Stone 47), the parse tree on one line
// ('tree', Stone 48), or the checker's verdict ('check',
// 'check-bare', 'check-guest', Stone 50: lex, parse, then
// the checker walk above; an accepted program prints
// exactly "checked"). In this stone the three check modes
// all run the hosted walk (see the checker section's
// header). A problem is spoken on the error channel,
// leaving with the contract's parting numbers: 2 when the
// file itself can't be opened (or the mode is not one this
// program knows), 1 when the lexer refuses the program —
// or, in 'tree' and the check modes, when the parser does,
// or in the check modes when the checker does (a lex
// refusal is reported exactly as in 'tokens'; no partial
// output is printed either way).

let allArgs = arguments()
if length(allArgs) < 2 {
    sayerr("Give me a mode and a file, like: nifla-front tokens program.nifla")
    exitwith(2)
} else {
    if allArgs[0] != "tokens" {
        if allArgs[0] != "tree" {
            if allArgs[0] != "check" {
                if allArgs[0] != "check-bare" {
                    if allArgs[0] != "check-guest" {
                        sayerr("I don't know the mode '" + allArgs[0] + "'. The modes I know are 'tokens', 'tree', 'check', 'check-bare' and 'check-guest', like: nifla-front tokens program.nifla")
                        exitwith(2)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    let path = allArgs[1]
    let source = ""
    attempt {
        source = readfile(path)
    } if it fails {
        sayerr("I couldn't open the file '" + path + "'. Check the name and the folder, and try again.")
        exitwith(2)
    }
    let lexed = lexAll(source)
    if lexed.ok == false {
        sayerr(lexed.message)
        exitwith(1)
    } else {
        if allArgs[0] == "tokens" {
            let lines = []
            for t in lexed.value {
                append(lines, dumpToken(t))
            }
            say join(lines, "\n")
        } else {
            let parsed = parseProgramAll(lexed.value)
            if parsed.ok == false {
                sayerr(parsed.message)
                exitwith(1)
            } else {
                if allArgs[0] == "tree" {
                    say dumpProgram(parsed.value)
                } else {
                    let checked = chkCheckProgram(parsed.value)
                    if checked.ok == false {
                        sayerr(checked.message)
                        exitwith(1)
                    } else {
                        say "checked"
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
